-Crow made this, uh,
batch of brownies here,

46
00:01:54,528 --> 00:01:56,219
and, uh, we're gonna
be enjoying those.

47
00:01:56,254 --> 00:01:58,083
Uh, we ran out of milk, though.

48
00:01:58,118 --> 00:01:59,774
We'll probably have
to use Tang...

49
00:01:59,809 --> 00:02:00,810
-Ick.
-...to drink with them.

50
00:02:00,844 --> 00:02:02,743
-Commercial sign in 5...

51
00:02:02,777 --> 00:02:04,917
4...3...2.

52
00:02:04,952 --> 00:02:06,781
Commercial sign now.
-Uh, don't go away.

53
00:02:06,816 --> 00:02:07,679
We'll be right back.

54
00:02:07,713 --> 00:02:08,783
If you do have to go away,

55
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:10,544
just come back
in a couple of minutes.

56
00:02:10,578 --> 00:02:14,168
Uh, my guest will be
Tom Servo and the amazing Crow.

57
00:02:14,203 --> 00:02:18,586
♪♪

58
00:02:45,786 --> 00:02:48,685
-Oh, man, Crow,
these were great brownies,

59
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:50,170
but we haven't
had brownie fixings

60
00:02:50,204 --> 00:02:51,447
around here for a long time.

61
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:53,690
What were they?
-Uh, uh, uh, hey.

62
00:02:53,725 --> 00:02:54,933
Is that a new jumpsuit, Joel?

63
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:56,935
Oh, we got, uh,
the scientists calling.

64
00:02:56,969 --> 00:02:58,212
-Phew.
-Look oppressed.

65
00:02:58,247 --> 00:03:03,217
-Come in, Joel,
my little bagel with a schmear.

66
00:03:03,252 --> 00:03:05,392
-Hey, sirs. Uh,
w-what's up?

67
00:03:05,426 --> 00:03:07,428
-Our income if
this new gig works out.

68
00:03:07,463 --> 00:03:09,430
-Yes, Larry and I have
developed a new chain

69
00:03:09,465 --> 00:03:12,019
of fast-food restaurants
with very low overhead

70
00:03:12,053 --> 00:03:13,641
because we don't cook our food.

71
00:03:13,676 --> 00:03:14,918
-'Cause frying and broiling

72
00:03:14,953 --> 00:03:16,920
takes out a lot
of the nutrients.

73
00:03:16,955 --> 00:03:19,647
-Yes, uh, if your body
likes another body,

74
00:03:19,682 --> 00:03:22,443
why don't you try one
of our burgers, au natural.

75
00:03:22,478 --> 00:03:26,171
It's, uh, ripped from the bone
to your plate in seconds.

76
00:03:26,206 --> 00:03:28,863
It's Clay and Lar's Flesh Barn.

77
00:03:28,898 --> 00:03:30,693
[Both laugh]Uh, make with the lyrics, Larry.

78
00:03:30,727 --> 00:03:32,108
-Okay.

79
00:03:34,283 --> 00:03:36,630
♪ If you're tired
of the same-old fare ♪

80
00:03:36,664 --> 00:03:39,149
♪ You got a friend
in Clay and Lar ♪

81
00:03:39,184 --> 00:03:41,462
♪ All our meat
is guaranteed rare ♪

82
00:03:41,497 --> 00:03:42,981
♪ Because we don't cook it

83
00:03:43,015 --> 00:03:45,984
-You see, cooking takes out
all the flavor.

84
00:03:46,018 --> 00:03:48,676
-♪ If you're tired
of cooking at home ♪

85
00:03:48,711 --> 00:03:51,127
♪ Try our meat
right off the bone ♪

86
00:03:51,161 --> 00:03:53,267
♪ If you listen,
you can hear it moan ♪

87
00:03:53,302 --> 00:03:55,096
♪ Because we don't cook it

88
00:03:55,131 --> 00:03:57,271
-Stunned, killed
right at your table,

89
00:03:57,306 --> 00:03:58,962
eviscerated very fresh.

90
00:03:58,997 --> 00:04:01,827
-♪ Now, there's no need
for you to drive through ♪

91
00:04:01,862 --> 00:04:04,105
♪ Our fresh meat
will walk out to you ♪

92
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:06,107
♪ You'll say, "Hi,"
he'll say, "Moo" ♪

93
00:04:06,142 --> 00:04:08,420
♪ It's Clay and Lar's
Flesh Barn ♪

94
00:04:08,455 --> 00:04:12,148
-15 locations to serve you,
now in Altoona.

95
00:04:12,182 --> 00:04:13,943
-Oh, well, uh, what
I have this week is,

96
00:04:13,977 --> 00:04:15,531
I was just -- got --
got kind of bored

97
00:04:15,565 --> 00:04:17,153
in the bathroom,
and I made this.

98
00:04:17,187 --> 00:04:18,223
It's, uh --

99
00:04:18,258 --> 00:04:21,226
As you can see,
it's, uh --

100
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:24,919
built a roll of toilet paper
in a 2-liter bottle.

101
00:04:24,954 --> 00:04:27,128
What do you think, sirs?

102
00:04:27,163 --> 00:04:28,889
-Great.
-Yeah.

103
00:04:28,923 --> 00:04:31,201
-You could use it
for a Molotov cocktail

104
00:04:31,236 --> 00:04:32,651
or something.
[Laughs]

105
00:04:32,686 --> 00:04:34,239
-Aw, you guys twist everything.

106
00:04:34,274 --> 00:04:36,414
You could've made
tiddlywinks evil.

107
00:04:36,448 --> 00:04:38,036
-Thank you!

108
00:04:38,070 --> 00:04:41,488
Well, your film this week,
Joel, is a little number

109
00:04:41,522 --> 00:04:43,904
called "Women of
the Prehistoric Planet."

110
00:04:43,938 --> 00:04:45,630
There's really no reason for it.

111
00:04:45,664 --> 00:04:48,149
It just is what it is.

112
00:04:48,184 --> 00:04:49,875
Deal with it, musky breath.

113
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:51,118
-Enjoy!

114
00:04:51,152 --> 00:04:52,292
[Rumbling, alarm blares]
-Oh, my God!

115
00:04:52,326 --> 00:04:53,431
-Whoa!

116
00:04:53,465 --> 00:04:55,502
-Aah!

117
00:05:02,371 --> 00:05:07,134
♪♪

118
00:05:07,168 --> 00:05:11,311
I still think that toilet paper
was a good idea.

119
00:05:11,345 --> 00:05:12,450
-I just don't understand it.

120
00:05:12,484 --> 00:05:13,934
-"Women of
the Prehistoric Planet."

121
00:05:13,968 --> 00:05:15,970
My sister saw this
in junior high.

122
00:05:16,005 --> 00:05:18,145
All the boys had
to go into the gym.

123
00:05:18,179 --> 00:05:20,596
♪♪

124
00:05:20,630 --> 00:05:22,425
-Wendell Corey.

125
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:24,876
-Man among men.

126
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:27,948
-John Agar.
-We wish you a Merry Anders.

127
00:05:29,398 --> 00:05:30,571
-Hmm.

128
00:05:31,607 --> 00:05:33,091
-Let's see.

129
00:05:33,125 --> 00:05:34,299
Kam Tong.

130
00:05:34,334 --> 00:05:35,852
Kam Tong races.
-Oh.

131
00:05:35,887 --> 00:05:37,440
You are here.

132
00:05:37,475 --> 00:05:38,441
-Yep.

133
00:05:38,476 --> 00:05:45,966
♪♪

134
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,209
-Oh, what's that, Servo?

135
00:05:48,244 --> 00:05:49,625
-I think it's the...

136
00:05:49,659 --> 00:05:52,075
creamy nougat center
of the Milky Way, uh, galaxy.

137
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:53,353
-Oh.

138
00:05:53,387 --> 00:05:56,873
-There's the caramel
constellation.

139
00:05:56,908 --> 00:05:58,772
-It's so beautiful.

140
00:06:01,153 --> 00:06:04,916
Looks like crud on a windshield.

141
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:07,263
-It's a cosmic sneeze.

142
00:06:07,297 --> 00:06:09,921
-That -- That's beautiful.

143
00:06:09,955 --> 00:06:19,137
♪♪

144
00:06:19,171 --> 00:06:20,966
Hey, look, it's,
uh, Lady Remington.

145
00:06:21,001 --> 00:06:22,347
-Looks like tin foil.

146
00:06:22,382 --> 00:06:24,349
-It's a steam iron.

147
00:06:24,384 --> 00:06:26,731
-Nah, it's a biscuit warmer.

148
00:06:26,765 --> 00:06:29,216
-It's a, uh, chrome watermelon.

149
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:31,045
-It's a metal football.

150
00:06:31,080 --> 00:06:33,462
♪♪

151
00:06:33,496 --> 00:06:36,016
-It's one of Gallagher's hats.

152
00:06:36,050 --> 00:06:38,984
-It's any piece of alternative
reality we choose it to be.

153
00:06:39,019 --> 00:06:41,331
-That's no alternative
piece of reality.

154
00:06:41,366 --> 00:06:42,816
That's a battle station!

155
00:06:42,850 --> 00:06:45,991
-Make an entry in the log book.

156
00:06:46,026 --> 00:06:47,545
-A big one.

157
00:06:48,787 --> 00:06:50,651
-On this day,

158
00:06:50,686 --> 00:06:52,481
lead command ship Cosmos I...

159
00:06:52,515 --> 00:06:55,484
-You know, he must hit his head
on that beam all the time.

160
00:06:55,518 --> 00:06:57,555
-...6 months, 18 days...

161
00:06:57,589 --> 00:06:59,419
-Probably doesn't
even feel it anymore.

162
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:01,559
-Cosmos IIand III

163
00:07:01,593 --> 00:07:04,424
are following in three-month
intervals, as programmed.

164
00:07:04,458 --> 00:07:05,804
-Looks like a great,
big Erector Set.

165
00:07:05,839 --> 00:07:07,772
-We continue homeward bound

166
00:07:07,806 --> 00:07:09,187
without incident.

167
00:07:09,221 --> 00:07:11,051
-Talking to ourselves.

168
00:07:11,085 --> 00:07:12,880
-Well, Doc, you better dust
off your dress uniform.

169
00:07:12,915 --> 00:07:14,813
-The strapless one?
-You'll soon be home.

170
00:07:14,848 --> 00:07:15,849
-Ha.

171
00:07:15,883 --> 00:07:16,988
Commander,
you've been saying that

172
00:07:17,022 --> 00:07:18,196
for the past six months.

173
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:19,818
-That's the biggest
problematic bubble

174
00:07:19,853 --> 00:07:21,233
I think I've ever seen.

175
00:07:21,268 --> 00:07:24,340
-Nah, I think it looks likea big Joe Namath Popcorn Popper.

176
00:07:24,374 --> 00:07:25,790
-Ba-dum.
-Positive.

177
00:07:25,824 --> 00:07:27,895
-That's the biggest
guitar I've ever seen

178
00:07:27,930 --> 00:07:29,483
built right into the wall
behind them.

179
00:07:29,518 --> 00:07:31,658
-...32 minutes to go.

180
00:07:31,692 --> 00:07:33,349
-Might as well be a year.

181
00:07:33,383 --> 00:07:36,352
That's how long it seems
since we left Centaurus.

182
00:07:36,386 --> 00:07:38,527
-Thanks for the exposition.
-Excuse me, Doc.

183
00:07:38,561 --> 00:07:41,875
That one month and 12 days
Commander Scott just mentioned,

184
00:07:41,909 --> 00:07:44,981
is that our time here
in space or time at home?

185
00:07:45,016 --> 00:07:46,500
-Uh, you're new here,
aren't you, Elvis?

186
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:49,296
-It's about six years back home.

187
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:50,780
-Six more years.

188
00:07:50,815 --> 00:07:52,299
Brother.
-Thank you, Colonel.

189
00:07:52,333 --> 00:07:53,473
-You know something, sir.

190
00:07:53,507 --> 00:07:55,509
We've been gone so long,
my kid brother is now

191
00:07:55,544 --> 00:07:57,200
30 years older than me.

192
00:07:57,235 --> 00:07:59,617
[Chuckles]
Is that crazy?

193
00:07:59,651 --> 00:08:02,585
-That's what happens whenyou travel at near optic speeds.

194
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:05,139
-It's kooky, Colonel.

195
00:08:05,174 --> 00:08:07,901
-I suppose in some ways,
it is crazy.

196
00:08:07,935 --> 00:08:09,040
-Imagine what would happen

197
00:08:09,074 --> 00:08:11,042
if a married guy came home
after five years

198
00:08:11,076 --> 00:08:12,664
and found his wife
was an old woman.

199
00:08:12,699 --> 00:08:15,218
-Oh, I don't even
wanna think about that!

200
00:08:15,253 --> 00:08:17,048
-It's not funny, Corporal.

201
00:08:17,082 --> 00:08:19,533
-...can't qualifyfor cosmic expedition, Sergeant.

202
00:08:19,568 --> 00:08:21,742
[Devices beeping]

203
00:08:21,777 --> 00:08:23,675
Distress signal from Cosmos III.

204
00:08:23,710 --> 00:08:25,263
-You have your coordinates,
Mr. Scott.

205
00:08:25,297 --> 00:08:27,161
Try to get an accurate fix
on their position.

206
00:08:27,196 --> 00:08:28,818
-I'll work on getting
these chicks hatched.

207
00:08:28,853 --> 00:08:30,855
-Communications -- King.

208
00:08:30,889 --> 00:08:32,822
-Uh, communications queen.

209
00:08:32,857 --> 00:08:35,100
Come in.
-...Cosmos III.

210
00:08:35,135 --> 00:08:37,171
-Yes, sir.
Clearing all channels.

211
00:08:37,206 --> 00:08:38,587
-Yes, your highness.

212
00:08:38,621 --> 00:08:40,520
-I have a fix on Cosmos III,
Admiral.

213
00:08:40,554 --> 00:08:42,867
-How do you barre chord again?

214
00:08:42,901 --> 00:08:44,627
-They're here in
the Solaris star system.

215
00:08:44,662 --> 00:08:47,872
-They're over by
E minor or C sharp.

216
00:08:47,906 --> 00:08:51,427
-It could be a dangerous area
to be off-course.

217
00:08:51,461 --> 00:08:53,118
Solaris is a new star

218
00:08:53,153 --> 00:08:55,604
surrounded by numerous planets

219
00:08:55,638 --> 00:08:57,813
and a great deal of cosmic gas.

220
00:08:57,847 --> 00:08:59,573
-Must be from the cosmic chili.

221
00:08:59,608 --> 00:09:01,437
-...gas clouds
at full acceleration,

222
00:09:01,471 --> 00:09:02,680
they'll burn up in an instant.

223
00:09:02,714 --> 00:09:04,854
-And we'll be there to watch.

224
00:09:04,889 --> 00:09:06,960
[Beeping continues]

225
00:09:06,994 --> 00:09:10,273
-Communications,
anything from Cosmos III?

226
00:09:10,308 --> 00:09:13,000
-Nothing yet, Admiral.
We have all channels wide open.

227
00:09:13,035 --> 00:09:16,279
-Try to contact them.
I'll be right in.

228
00:09:16,314 --> 00:09:18,765
Sergeant, reduce speed 1/8th.

229
00:09:18,799 --> 00:09:20,594
-And try to pretend
that meant something.

230
00:09:20,629 --> 00:09:23,942
-There's no sense in
getting the gap between us

231
00:09:23,977 --> 00:09:25,357
any wider than possible.

232
00:09:25,392 --> 00:09:26,358
-Yes, sir.
-The what?

233
00:09:26,393 --> 00:09:28,775
-The gap between our ears.

234
00:09:28,809 --> 00:09:32,019
-Prepare to reduce programmed
acceleration by 1/8th.

235
00:09:32,054 --> 00:09:34,194
-Think they're gonna
be the comic relief?

236
00:09:34,228 --> 00:09:36,921
I just got a feeling.

237
00:09:36,955 --> 00:09:38,854
Did you hear what I heard,
Mr. Bradley?

238
00:09:38,888 --> 00:09:41,201
We're reducing speed.
-I heard.

239
00:09:41,235 --> 00:09:42,892
-They're pretty well-groomed
for having been in space

240
00:09:42,927 --> 00:09:44,791
for so long.
-...all this time.

241
00:09:44,825 --> 00:09:46,068
-Not with Scott navigating.

242
00:09:46,102 --> 00:09:47,656
He knows where we were,
where we are,

243
00:09:47,690 --> 00:09:49,727
and where we're going to be
at any given moment --

244
00:09:49,761 --> 00:09:52,005
past, present, or future.

245
00:09:52,039 --> 00:09:53,523
-And all from
the horoscope charts

246
00:09:53,558 --> 00:09:56,630
he got from Nancy Reagan.

247
00:09:56,665 --> 00:09:58,114
-I'll, uh...
I'll check it out.

248
00:09:58,149 --> 00:09:59,668
Stand by, Chief.

249
00:10:02,222 --> 00:10:03,844
-Activate maneuver.

250
00:10:05,984 --> 00:10:07,676
-[Clicking tongue]

251
00:10:07,710 --> 00:10:10,195
-Now, don't tell me
he's thinking of turning back.

252
00:10:10,230 --> 00:10:11,334
-Uh, he's thinking
of turning back.

253
00:10:11,369 --> 00:10:13,026
-What would you do?
-Turn back.

254
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:14,096
-There are 20 people

255
00:10:14,130 --> 00:10:15,718
and the valuable cargo
of gravitide

256
00:10:15,753 --> 00:10:17,306
aboard that ship.

257
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:19,135
There is responsibility.

258
00:10:19,170 --> 00:10:20,550
Correction, Doc.

259
00:10:20,585 --> 00:10:23,657
There are 12 men and the cargo
aboard the Cosmos III.

260
00:10:23,692 --> 00:10:25,176
The rest are Centaurians.

261
00:10:25,210 --> 00:10:27,178
-Oh, yes, yes.
-They're all wearing Dickies.

262
00:10:27,212 --> 00:10:28,386
-You, uh...
You don't consider

263
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:30,940
Centaurians people,
do you, Scott?

264
00:10:30,975 --> 00:10:33,046
-I don't think we should put so
much trust in them, that's all.

265
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:36,290
-Let's lock them all
in relocation camps.

266
00:10:36,325 --> 00:10:37,637
-Hey, it's Pink Lady.

267
00:10:37,671 --> 00:10:40,501
-Centaurians once had
a very high culture, Scott.

268
00:10:40,536 --> 00:10:43,159
-And Jeff.-They could again with our help.

269
00:10:43,194 --> 00:10:44,816
-You sound exactly
like the Admiral.

270
00:10:44,851 --> 00:10:46,438
-And you sound
like Archie Bunker.

271
00:10:46,473 --> 00:10:49,683
-...here so long, your sense
of values have changed.

272
00:10:49,718 --> 00:10:51,685
-A deep-space colony
can't always live

273
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:54,067
by the standards
we do back home.

274
00:10:54,101 --> 00:10:56,897
-Admiral King's theory
of cosmic colonization.

275
00:10:56,932 --> 00:10:58,174
-Is that a Ray Stevens song?

276
00:10:58,209 --> 00:11:01,212
-It was required reading
at the academy, Dr. Farrell.

277
00:11:01,246 --> 00:11:04,767
Commander, if you try
getting to know the admiral,

278
00:11:04,802 --> 00:11:07,701
you might discover
he's quite a remarkable man.

279
00:11:07,736 --> 00:11:10,704
-And really quite stunning
when you think about it.

280
00:11:10,739 --> 00:11:13,396
-I've been with him on Centaurusfor the past three years.

281
00:11:13,431 --> 00:11:15,398
-La-de-da.-I think I know him pretty well.

282
00:11:15,433 --> 00:11:18,125
-And you're no Jack Kennedy,
senator.

283
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:19,264
-...almost 20 years.

284
00:11:19,299 --> 00:11:21,646
-Infectious.
-I belive I know him better.

285
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:24,614
-Yeah, well, my dad
can beat up your dad.

286
00:11:24,649 --> 00:11:26,651
Ha!

287
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:28,722
-Cosmos fleet command ship one

288
00:11:28,757 --> 00:11:29,999
to Cosmos III.

289
00:11:30,034 --> 00:11:33,278
-Disinflating Annette's hair,
starting on my mark.

290
00:11:33,313 --> 00:11:36,005
-...if you are receiving,
please acknowledge.

291
00:11:40,078 --> 00:11:41,942
-Shall we keep trying, sir?

292
00:11:43,668 --> 00:11:46,706
-Cosmos III,it's Admiral King.

293
00:11:46,740 --> 00:11:50,295
If you are receiving,
please advise Captain Ross

294
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:53,713
that he's off-course and
approaching a dangerous area.

295
00:11:53,747 --> 00:11:54,955
Repeat --

296
00:11:54,990 --> 00:11:58,165
Your present course will
take you into a danger zone.

297
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,580
-What?

298
00:11:59,615 --> 00:12:01,513
-Keep trying.

299
00:12:01,548 --> 00:12:03,757
Repeat the same message
on all the channels.

300
00:12:03,792 --> 00:12:06,208
-In your voice, sir?
-Admiral King.

301
00:12:06,242 --> 00:12:08,348
Do you think something
has happened to them?

302
00:12:08,382 --> 00:12:10,212
-We don't know yet, Linda.

303
00:12:10,246 --> 00:12:13,215
-I have friends aboard
the Cosmos III.

304
00:12:13,249 --> 00:12:15,148
I am afraid for them.

305
00:12:15,182 --> 00:12:18,220
-We all have friends
on Cosmos III.

306
00:12:18,254 --> 00:12:19,773
We're all deeply concerned.

307
00:12:19,808 --> 00:12:22,983
-So concerned that I felt
compelled to touch you.

308
00:12:26,815 --> 00:12:30,301
-Cosmos fleet command ship one
to Cosmos III.

309
00:12:30,335 --> 00:12:34,857
-Pigs...in...spaaace!

310
00:12:37,929 --> 00:12:40,069
-Great gag.
-Cosmos Ito Cosmos III.

311
00:12:40,104 --> 00:12:44,418
If you can transmit,
please acknowledge.

312
00:12:44,453 --> 00:12:45,730
-Jang,
you've got to listen to me.

313
00:12:45,765 --> 00:12:46,904
Both of you.

314
00:12:46,938 --> 00:12:49,492
Put away those guns.
It'll soon be too late.

315
00:12:49,527 --> 00:12:50,493
-No.

316
00:12:50,528 --> 00:12:52,633
-No way, old man.

317
00:12:52,668 --> 00:12:55,947
-Can't you understand,
this ship is out of control!

318
00:12:55,982 --> 00:12:58,363
Unless you release those men
in the maneuvering room,

319
00:12:58,398 --> 00:13:00,124
we're going nowhere
but to our death!

320
00:13:00,158 --> 00:13:01,297
-You're trying to trick us,
Captain.

321
00:13:01,332 --> 00:13:03,437
-I am not trying to --

322
00:13:03,472 --> 00:13:05,405
-You, Lieutenant. Anderson.

323
00:13:05,439 --> 00:13:07,752
You will take us
back to Centaurus.

324
00:13:07,787 --> 00:13:10,928
-You stupid fools.
-It's pronounced Centaurus.

325
00:13:10,962 --> 00:13:13,344
-He said the Centaurians
couldn't be trusted.

326
00:13:13,378 --> 00:13:14,448
-Jang, what are you doing?

327
00:13:14,483 --> 00:13:16,485
-Think of us as Klingons
without the Kling.

328
00:13:16,519 --> 00:13:18,004
-...turning back to Centaurus.

329
00:13:18,038 --> 00:13:19,522
-Chang, this is treasonous.

330
00:13:19,557 --> 00:13:21,697
Give me the gun.

331
00:13:21,731 --> 00:13:23,526
[Rumbling]

332
00:13:23,561 --> 00:13:25,943
-A very strong
magnetic field, sir.

333
00:13:25,977 --> 00:13:26,944
We're locked in.

334
00:13:26,978 --> 00:13:28,669
-It's one of the planets
of Solaris.

335
00:13:28,704 --> 00:13:30,775
We've entered
it's field of gravity.

336
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:32,570
-Activate the decelerator,
Sergeant.

337
00:13:32,604 --> 00:13:33,882
-What does it mean, Captain?

338
00:13:33,916 --> 00:13:35,262
-It's no use.

339
00:13:35,297 --> 00:13:37,644
-It means that we're doomed --

340
00:13:37,678 --> 00:13:39,749
crash into that planet
unless I can regain control

341
00:13:39,784 --> 00:13:40,820
of the maneuvering room.

342
00:13:40,854 --> 00:13:42,822
-All: Not again!

343
00:13:42,856 --> 00:13:44,996
-And that playground ball
is a threat, too.

344
00:13:45,031 --> 00:13:47,033
-Then we shall crash.

345
00:13:47,067 --> 00:13:48,379
It is better that we die...

346
00:13:48,413 --> 00:13:50,139
-Woop.
-...than be your slaves.

347
00:13:50,174 --> 00:13:51,796
-We have never treated you
as a slave.

348
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:53,522
-Yeah, what about that one time?

349
00:13:53,556 --> 00:13:55,524
-The captain knows
one of the b--

350
00:13:55,558 --> 00:13:57,112
-Imperial dog.

351
00:13:57,146 --> 00:13:59,114
-...activate
the gravity compensator!

352
00:13:59,148 --> 00:14:03,014
♪♪

353
00:14:03,049 --> 00:14:07,087
-We're gonna crash
into a Universal picture!

354
00:14:07,122 --> 00:14:11,609
♪♪

355
00:14:38,463 --> 00:14:44,331
♪♪

356
00:14:44,366 --> 00:14:46,471
-Fakey.

357
00:14:46,506 --> 00:14:49,854
♪♪

358
00:14:49,889 --> 00:14:52,339
-All: Fakey.

359
00:14:52,374 --> 00:14:54,859
-Real.

360
00:14:54,894 --> 00:14:55,998
-Dead guy.

361
00:14:56,033 --> 00:14:57,758
-All: Fakey.

362
00:14:57,793 --> 00:15:00,382
-Real.

363
00:15:00,416 --> 00:15:01,624
-Real fakey.

364
00:15:01,659 --> 00:15:03,281
-Fake guys.

365
00:15:03,316 --> 00:15:06,629
-Filmed on location,
in Tommy Ronick's front yard.

366
00:15:06,664 --> 00:15:12,221
♪♪

367
00:15:12,256 --> 00:15:17,778
♪♪

368
00:15:17,813 --> 00:15:18,883
-They're hammered.

369
00:15:18,918 --> 00:15:20,436
-And the chicks are dead.

370
00:15:20,471 --> 00:15:23,267
[Whirring]

371
00:15:23,301 --> 00:15:25,786
-Bzzz.

372
00:15:25,821 --> 00:15:27,202
-What do you suppose
happened to them, Admiral?

373
00:15:27,236 --> 00:15:29,169
-Ah, they probably crashed
in Tommy's front yard.

374
00:15:29,204 --> 00:15:31,068
-Magnetic fields.

375
00:15:31,102 --> 00:15:32,241
Cosmic clouds.

376
00:15:32,276 --> 00:15:34,243
-Yellow moons, pinks hearts,

377
00:15:34,278 --> 00:15:35,520
all that stuff.

378
00:15:35,555 --> 00:15:37,384
-It shouldn't.

379
00:15:37,419 --> 00:15:39,041
-I wonder how they got
so far off course.

380
00:15:39,076 --> 00:15:42,113
I mean, it's impossible
they didn't know.

381
00:15:42,148 --> 00:15:43,908
-No question about it, Admiral.

382
00:15:43,943 --> 00:15:46,531
They're grounded on one of
the inner planets of Solaris.

383
00:15:46,566 --> 00:15:47,843
-Yikes.
-Communications.

384
00:15:47,877 --> 00:15:49,017
Anything coming in?

385
00:15:49,051 --> 00:15:51,019
-No, Admiral.
Nothing but the homing signal.

386
00:15:51,053 --> 00:15:53,745
-Any reply from
Command Headquarters?

387
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:56,127
-Nothing yet, Admiral.
-Keep me informed.

388
00:15:56,162 --> 00:15:58,992
-Well, that's not gonna be easy.You're kind of a muttonhead.

389
00:15:59,027 --> 00:16:00,683
[Devices beeping]

390
00:16:02,996 --> 00:16:05,309
-The admiral is really upset,
isn't he?

391
00:16:05,343 --> 00:16:06,172
-Hmm.
-Yeah.

392
00:16:06,206 --> 00:16:07,794
-First ship he's ever lost.

393
00:16:07,828 --> 00:16:10,486
-Lost his keys a few times,
but never a whole ship.

394
00:16:10,521 --> 00:16:12,419
-Do you think
he would turn back?

395
00:16:12,454 --> 00:16:13,731
-Probably.
He's kind of a coward.

396
00:16:13,765 --> 00:16:15,181
-If High Command will let him,
he will.

397
00:16:15,215 --> 00:16:17,735
-Hmm. I don't think so.

398
00:16:17,769 --> 00:16:19,840
-We're too close to home.

399
00:16:19,875 --> 00:16:22,326
-You know we'll never get there.

400
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:24,776
It is just a wishful dream.

401
00:16:24,811 --> 00:16:26,606
-I know what you mean, honey.
-Uh-oh.

402
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:28,263
-Six months of all
this pressurized...

403
00:16:28,297 --> 00:16:29,712
-Moron patrol.

404
00:16:29,747 --> 00:16:32,888
-...isn't exactly
what I'd call a pleasure party.

405
00:16:32,922 --> 00:16:35,822
-Well, it could be, sugar,
if you'd let yourself go.

406
00:16:35,856 --> 00:16:38,204
-Well, well,
the wolf of outer space.

407
00:16:38,238 --> 00:16:40,896
And who let you out
of your darkened den?

408
00:16:40,930 --> 00:16:42,277
-Now, be kind to me, ladies.

409
00:16:42,311 --> 00:16:44,624
Don't forget that
I'm your ingenious engineer,

410
00:16:44,658 --> 00:16:46,281
the master
of the maneuvering room

411
00:16:46,315 --> 00:16:48,455
and the activator
of your gravitators.

412
00:16:48,490 --> 00:16:50,354
-Not to mention
a flagellating butthead.

413
00:16:50,388 --> 00:16:51,976
-...be no ups or downs.

414
00:16:52,011 --> 00:16:53,667
-Here we go again.

415
00:16:53,702 --> 00:16:57,154
-Well, I suppose we are gratefulfor all these amazing services,

416
00:16:57,188 --> 00:16:58,431
Mr. Bradley.

417
00:16:58,465 --> 00:17:00,985
-Lieutenant, you just allow
this sweet thing

418
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:02,780
a few moments liberty
with your old star mate,

419
00:17:02,814 --> 00:17:04,471
and, uh...

420
00:17:04,506 --> 00:17:07,060
well, I'll consider
you all paid in full.

421
00:17:07,095 --> 00:17:10,098
-He's done everything,
but insult a Centaurian.

422
00:17:10,132 --> 00:17:12,169
-N-Now, Lieutenant,
there's no reason

423
00:17:12,203 --> 00:17:14,516
for you to treat me like
a common old Centaurian.

424
00:17:14,550 --> 00:17:16,759
-Oh.

425
00:17:16,794 --> 00:17:17,622
-Oops.

426
00:17:17,657 --> 00:17:19,521
-Hey, Linda, I'm sorry.

427
00:17:19,555 --> 00:17:21,868
Well, now, you know I didn't
mean anything by what I said.

428
00:17:21,902 --> 00:17:23,525
-Nooo.

429
00:17:23,559 --> 00:17:26,217
♪♪

430
00:17:26,252 --> 00:17:28,909
-You know me,
I could light up a whole room.

431
00:17:28,944 --> 00:17:30,221
[Laughs]

432
00:17:30,256 --> 00:17:31,912
Just by walking out.

433
00:17:31,947 --> 00:17:34,915
-Yaaay.

434
00:17:34,950 --> 00:17:37,090
♪♪

435
00:17:37,125 --> 00:17:39,161
-Forget it, Linda.
He didn't mean it.

436
00:17:39,196 --> 00:17:40,507
-You know, she's right.

437
00:17:40,542 --> 00:17:42,785
I mean, Lieutenant Bradley's
crazy about you.

438
00:17:42,820 --> 00:17:45,616
-Hey, check out those
space-age lockers.

439
00:17:45,650 --> 00:17:47,859
-You know that.

440
00:17:47,894 --> 00:17:49,999
-Yes, I know.

441
00:17:50,034 --> 00:17:54,142
But I wonder if your people
really canaccept us, Karen.

442
00:17:54,176 --> 00:17:55,695
-Listen, Linda,

443
00:17:55,729 --> 00:17:58,111
y-you have an exciting
new life ahead of you.

444
00:17:58,146 --> 00:18:01,218
Why, don't worry
about being accepted.

445
00:18:01,252 --> 00:18:02,667
-'Cause you won't be.

446
00:18:02,702 --> 00:18:04,807
-When you get around all those
young cadets at the academy,

447
00:18:04,842 --> 00:18:06,430
you'll forget
all about Centaurus

448
00:18:06,464 --> 00:18:08,328
and those years in the colony

449
00:18:08,363 --> 00:18:11,193
and people with narrow minds.

450
00:18:11,228 --> 00:18:12,884
-Among other things.

451
00:18:12,919 --> 00:18:15,404
-You know, I've never even
see a boy of my own age.

452
00:18:15,439 --> 00:18:16,819
-I'm resetting the controls

453
00:18:16,854 --> 00:18:18,925
so they crash into
the prehistoric planet.

454
00:18:18,959 --> 00:18:20,927
-Command headquarters.

455
00:18:20,961 --> 00:18:22,480
[Chiming]

456
00:18:31,386 --> 00:18:33,112
[Device beeps]

457
00:18:33,146 --> 00:18:36,115
-Do I look good up here?

458
00:18:36,149 --> 00:18:39,290
-The reply from Space Command,
Mr. Scott.

459
00:18:39,325 --> 00:18:42,707
-It says, "Do not tear,
spindle, or mutilate."

460
00:18:44,226 --> 00:18:47,471
-Communique from Headquarters,
Admiral.

461
00:18:47,505 --> 00:18:49,404
-Looks like your agent's not
gonna get you out of this one.

462
00:18:49,438 --> 00:18:52,303
-"Since no other communications
can be established,

463
00:18:52,338 --> 00:18:54,754
it must be assumed
that Cosmos IIIhas crashed,

464
00:18:54,788 --> 00:18:56,583
killing all crew members
and passengers."

465
00:18:56,618 --> 00:18:57,895
-He's right.

466
00:18:57,929 --> 00:19:01,140
-"Regret your requestto return to star system Solaris

467
00:19:01,174 --> 00:19:04,660
to search for survivors
must be denied."

468
00:19:04,695 --> 00:19:06,869
-Signed by Zippy the Chimp.

469
00:19:06,904 --> 00:19:09,009
-Sorry, sir.
-Huh?

470
00:19:09,044 --> 00:19:11,702
-It would've been
a futile effort, Admiral.

471
00:19:11,736 --> 00:19:15,050
-We will reverse our course,
Mr. Scott.

472
00:19:15,084 --> 00:19:17,984
Program our flight plan
to take us to Solaris.

473
00:19:18,018 --> 00:19:19,434
-But sir,
the request had been denied

474
00:19:19,468 --> 00:19:20,573
by Command Headquarters.

475
00:19:20,607 --> 00:19:22,678
-I'm the commander
of this fleet!

476
00:19:22,713 --> 00:19:25,060
All the passengers
and crewmen on these ships

477
00:19:25,094 --> 00:19:28,201
are my responsibilityas long as we are in deep space.

478
00:19:28,236 --> 00:19:31,031
We are still in space.

479
00:19:31,066 --> 00:19:32,688
-Well, they're
in deep something.

480
00:19:32,723 --> 00:19:35,415
-Is that clear, Mr. Scott?

481
00:19:35,450 --> 00:19:37,314
-Hold me.
-Yes, sir.

482
00:19:38,798 --> 00:19:39,937
-I mean Mr...

483
00:19:39,971 --> 00:19:41,352
-Get a communique
to Command Headquarters

484
00:19:41,387 --> 00:19:43,251
to that effect immediately.

485
00:19:46,495 --> 00:19:48,463
-Nevins, stand by
to reverse course.

486
00:19:48,497 --> 00:19:49,636
Horizontal maneuver.

487
00:19:49,671 --> 00:19:50,948
-Yes, sir.
-That's how he got that job.

488
00:19:50,982 --> 00:19:52,294
-Maneuvering room,
this is controls.

489
00:19:52,329 --> 00:19:55,055
Stand by horizontal activators
for a change in course.

490
00:19:55,090 --> 00:19:58,369
-And beam up more depth.
-Maneuvering room standing by.

491
00:19:58,404 --> 00:20:00,406
-I'll talk to you later, sugar.

492
00:20:00,440 --> 00:20:02,097
-I just made out on the phone.

493
00:20:02,131 --> 00:20:03,340
This is great!

494
00:20:03,374 --> 00:20:05,307
-Well, what is all this,
Mr. Bradley?

495
00:20:05,342 --> 00:20:06,584
First we slow down to a walk...

496
00:20:06,619 --> 00:20:08,897
-It's a control panel.
You should know that.

497
00:20:08,931 --> 00:20:11,071
-Looks like
we're going back, Chief.

498
00:20:11,106 --> 00:20:13,729
-You mean Space Command
okayed the old man's request?

499
00:20:13,764 --> 00:20:15,455
-Well, if they didn't,
he's sure gonna catch it

500
00:20:15,490 --> 00:20:16,801
when we doget home.

501
00:20:16,836 --> 00:20:18,562
-Maneuvering room from Control.

502
00:20:18,596 --> 00:20:21,254
New course is 1-7-5 degrees

503
00:20:21,289 --> 00:20:23,049
on horizontal plane.

504
00:20:23,083 --> 00:20:25,603
-1-7-5 horizontal.

505
00:20:25,638 --> 00:20:29,228
[Clacking]

506
00:20:29,262 --> 00:20:31,264
Program.
-That's all you have to do?

507
00:20:31,299 --> 00:20:33,439
I could have done that.
-...on Cosmos III'shoming beam

508
00:20:33,473 --> 00:20:35,130
for three month's cruising.

509
00:20:35,164 --> 00:20:37,201
Full acceleration.
-Three months?

510
00:20:37,236 --> 00:20:38,754
Ye-- Yes, sir.

511
00:20:40,308 --> 00:20:42,344
-I got this
Snoopy ruler for free.

512
00:20:42,379 --> 00:20:44,035
What do you think, Tom?

513
00:20:44,070 --> 00:20:45,278
Anybody?

514
00:20:45,313 --> 00:20:47,107
Somebody.
-Activate maneuver.

515
00:20:47,142 --> 00:20:53,252
♪♪

516
00:20:53,286 --> 00:20:55,702
-People always seem
to comfort her.

517
00:20:55,737 --> 00:21:00,362
♪♪

518
00:21:00,397 --> 00:21:03,227
-Even our name
says Merry Christmas!

519
00:21:03,262 --> 00:21:09,578
♪♪

520
00:21:09,613 --> 00:21:12,201
-Gilligan!
-Skipper!

521
00:21:12,236 --> 00:21:16,102
-Gilligan!
-Skipper!

522
00:21:16,136 --> 00:21:17,276
Mary Anne!

523
00:21:17,310 --> 00:21:19,726
-Ginger!
-Thurston?

524
00:21:19,761 --> 00:21:21,866
-Lovey!
-Wrong way.

525
00:21:21,901 --> 00:21:23,696
-Japanese guy!

526
00:21:23,730 --> 00:21:31,531
♪♪

527
00:21:31,566 --> 00:21:34,051
-Hey, the mummy's
getting better.

528
00:21:34,085 --> 00:21:37,054
♪♪

529
00:21:37,088 --> 00:21:38,745
[Bird squawking in distance]

530
00:21:38,780 --> 00:21:40,954
-We'll stop here for the night.

531
00:21:40,989 --> 00:21:42,749
At least it's dry ground.

532
00:21:42,784 --> 00:21:46,305
-Yeah, rest here.
Maybe we can find your scalp.

533
00:21:46,339 --> 00:21:47,720
Hey, here's some mossy lichen.

534
00:21:47,754 --> 00:21:49,411
We can make a wig
out of it for ya.

535
00:21:49,446 --> 00:21:51,102
-Don't touch me!

536
00:21:51,137 --> 00:21:52,794
I told you to stay away from me.

537
00:21:52,828 --> 00:21:54,347
-I only want to help you,
Lieutenant.

538
00:21:54,382 --> 00:21:55,383
You need help.

539
00:21:55,417 --> 00:21:56,556
-Yeah,
but you put my arm in a sling

540
00:21:56,591 --> 00:21:57,626
and it doesn't even hurt.

541
00:21:57,661 --> 00:21:59,870
-...none of us
wouldn't need any help.

542
00:21:59,904 --> 00:22:01,665
-I'm sorry for
what has happened,

543
00:22:01,699 --> 00:22:03,252
but Ididn't rebel.

544
00:22:03,287 --> 00:22:05,496
-Jang was your brother.
What's the difference?!

545
00:22:05,531 --> 00:22:06,980
-Jang thinks for himself.

546
00:22:07,015 --> 00:22:09,051
I'm not responsible.

547
00:22:09,086 --> 00:22:10,363
-You're all alike.

548
00:22:10,398 --> 00:22:13,366
You take everything from us,
and you give nothing.

549
00:22:13,401 --> 00:22:14,781
I hate all of you!

550
00:22:14,816 --> 00:22:17,957
-But, Sally, we were friends...
-Hi, I'm Johnny Longtorso.

551
00:22:17,991 --> 00:22:19,890
Can I be of any help here?

552
00:22:19,924 --> 00:22:22,582
-Leave me alone, please!

553
00:22:22,617 --> 00:22:25,274
♪♪

554
00:22:25,309 --> 00:22:26,897
-What's up, little actress.

555
00:22:26,931 --> 00:22:28,795
-Uh, maybe this
isn't a good time,

556
00:22:28,830 --> 00:22:30,556
but, uh, could you
launder my pants?

557
00:22:30,590 --> 00:22:31,522
-No, Zenda.

558
00:22:31,557 --> 00:22:33,386
I don't hate you,
but...[Sighs]

559
00:22:33,421 --> 00:22:35,975
-But you wish you'd never
heard of Centaurus --

560
00:22:36,009 --> 00:22:36,769
or me.

561
00:22:36,803 --> 00:22:38,115
-I didn't say that.

562
00:22:38,149 --> 00:22:40,082
-Uh, let's just say
that I don't hate you.

563
00:22:40,117 --> 00:22:41,877
I-I'm not gonna call you
for racquetball,

564
00:22:41,912 --> 00:22:43,534
if that's what you mean.

565
00:22:43,569 --> 00:22:49,954
♪♪

566
00:22:49,989 --> 00:22:52,612
-I wonder what she wanted.

567
00:22:52,647 --> 00:22:54,165
-Here's my card.

568
00:22:54,200 --> 00:22:57,376
Johnny Longtorso.

569
00:22:57,410 --> 00:22:58,963
-Here, Sally.

570
00:22:58,998 --> 00:22:59,930
-It's PEZ.

571
00:22:59,964 --> 00:23:02,657
Make you feel better.

572
00:23:02,691 --> 00:23:04,313
-It'll kill the pain.

573
00:23:04,348 --> 00:23:06,350
♪♪

574
00:23:06,385 --> 00:23:09,318
-And, uh, here --
It's my motorman's helper.

575
00:23:09,353 --> 00:23:12,460
-Finish it!

576
00:23:12,494 --> 00:23:14,945
-I wish I could do more.

577
00:23:14,979 --> 00:23:16,325
I wish I were a doctor.

578
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:18,120
-I wish I were a pixie,

579
00:23:18,155 --> 00:23:20,260
but let's get real.

580
00:23:20,295 --> 00:23:23,505
-You wished for her,
and you got her.

581
00:23:23,540 --> 00:23:24,817
-What did I get?

582
00:23:24,851 --> 00:23:26,025
Nothing.

583
00:23:26,059 --> 00:23:28,303
Well, I'm gonna try wishing!

584
00:23:28,337 --> 00:23:30,029
I wish I were dead!

585
00:23:30,063 --> 00:23:31,755
-That could be arranged.

586
00:23:31,789 --> 00:23:33,446
-We're gonna be alright.

587
00:23:33,481 --> 00:23:35,655
Admiral King will
come back for us.

588
00:23:35,690 --> 00:23:38,071
Cosmos I'sprobably on its way
here this very minute.

589
00:23:38,106 --> 00:23:39,659
-And hey, you got a sling
out of the deal,

590
00:23:39,694 --> 00:23:40,971
and it's a pretty neat one.

591
00:23:41,005 --> 00:23:43,905
-Even if they know where we are,

592
00:23:43,939 --> 00:23:46,563
Command Headquarters
will never let him turn back.

593
00:23:46,597 --> 00:23:49,738
-We're too annoying for anyone
to consider picking us up.

594
00:23:49,773 --> 00:23:52,707
It's over.
-I know Admiral King, too.

595
00:23:52,741 --> 00:23:55,813
Anyway,
we've got to hope, Sally.

596
00:23:55,848 --> 00:23:58,540
♪♪

597
00:23:58,575 --> 00:23:59,507
-Nice speech.

598
00:23:59,541 --> 00:24:01,543
Now can I get some sleep,

599
00:24:01,578 --> 00:24:04,822
for goodness sake?

600
00:24:04,857 --> 00:24:06,686
-Nice statue.
Oh! She's real.

601
00:24:06,721 --> 00:24:11,519
-Weren't you in Valley High
or Hawaiian High?

602
00:24:11,553 --> 00:24:13,969
-You'd better get some rest,
Zenda.

603
00:24:14,004 --> 00:24:15,108
We may have a long way to go

604
00:24:15,143 --> 00:24:17,732
before we get out
of this jungle.

605
00:24:17,766 --> 00:24:20,182
-Do you really believe
they'll be back to look for us?

606
00:24:20,217 --> 00:24:23,392
-Of course I do.

607
00:24:23,427 --> 00:24:25,291
-You're a good man, Charles.
-Brown.

608
00:24:25,325 --> 00:24:28,225
-Your faith and courage
even in defeat.

609
00:24:28,259 --> 00:24:29,951
-And my feet hurt like hell
right now.

610
00:24:29,985 --> 00:24:31,608
Ba-da-boom.
-We're still alive.

611
00:24:31,642 --> 00:24:33,506
♪♪

612
00:24:33,541 --> 00:24:36,475
-Tomorrow, we should be able
to reach the foothills.

613
00:24:36,509 --> 00:24:37,752
It'll be safer there.

614
00:24:37,786 --> 00:24:40,582
-If I read my script correctly,
that is.

615
00:24:40,617 --> 00:24:43,896
-...we can still
live on this planet...

616
00:24:43,930 --> 00:24:46,070
if we just trust each other.

617
00:24:46,105 --> 00:24:47,831
♪♪

618
00:24:47,865 --> 00:24:50,419
-We could play booblie-ooblie
for years.

619
00:24:50,454 --> 00:24:52,421
And the banana will stick.

620
00:24:52,456 --> 00:24:53,561
-Oh, we better get outta here.

621
00:24:53,595 --> 00:24:54,423
-Alright.

622
00:24:54,458 --> 00:24:56,218
-Wonder what she wanted.

623
00:24:56,253 --> 00:24:58,531
♪♪

624
00:24:58,566 --> 00:25:00,775
[Door creaks]

625
00:25:00,809 --> 00:25:02,673
[Whirring]

626
00:25:04,606 --> 00:25:06,781
[Clanging]

627
00:25:06,815 --> 00:25:10,129
-Joel! This is your life!

628
00:25:10,163 --> 00:25:13,235
[Applause effect]

629
00:25:13,270 --> 00:25:15,859
You're an ambitious young man
who began your life in space

630
00:25:15,893 --> 00:25:18,344
as a custodian
for Gizmonic Institute.

631
00:25:18,378 --> 00:25:20,242
Do you remember this voice?

632
00:25:20,277 --> 00:25:22,279
-There's an unidentified
satellite straying

633
00:25:22,313 --> 00:25:23,660
into our orbital path!

634
00:25:23,694 --> 00:25:24,661
-Oh, well, that's easy.

635
00:25:24,695 --> 00:25:26,421
It's, uh, my old friend Crow.

636
00:25:26,455 --> 00:25:28,285
I think he's talking
about the time we partied

637
00:25:28,319 --> 00:25:30,356
with the fellas from Salyut 7.

638
00:25:30,390 --> 00:25:32,600
-Uh! Uh! Joel!
Joel, help!

639
00:25:32,634 --> 00:25:34,498
Big satellite!
Big death!

640
00:25:34,533 --> 00:25:37,466
Danger, wreckage,
ouch, sting, pain.

641
00:25:37,501 --> 00:25:39,745
Hey, where did this couch
come from, anyway?

642
00:25:39,779 --> 00:25:41,263
-Uh, I-I found it
in one of the crates

643
00:25:41,298 --> 00:25:42,161
down in the loading bay.

644
00:25:42,195 --> 00:25:43,334
I thought I'd redecorate.

645
00:25:43,369 --> 00:25:45,233
What's this about
a death satellite?

646
00:25:45,267 --> 00:25:47,407
-Oh, yeah. Uh...big --
big death satellite.

647
00:25:47,442 --> 00:25:49,409
Oh, big! Pain.
Look, run.

648
00:25:49,444 --> 00:25:51,032
Help, help.
-Hey, just settle down.

649
00:25:51,066 --> 00:25:53,448
Come up here.
Come on up here and sit down.

650
00:25:53,482 --> 00:25:54,725
Tell me all about it, okay?

651
00:25:54,760 --> 00:25:56,002
Just take a few deep breaths.

652
00:25:56,037 --> 00:25:57,556
That's alright.

653
00:25:57,590 --> 00:26:00,489
-Well, there's, uh...
a big satellite out there

654
00:26:00,524 --> 00:26:02,388
and, uh, we're gonna
collide with it, and...

655
00:26:02,422 --> 00:26:03,562
-What?!
-Oh!

656
00:26:03,596 --> 00:26:04,977
[Cans clatter]
-Oh, Cambot,

657
00:26:05,011 --> 00:26:07,151
give me an exterior
of the ship, quick!

658
00:26:07,186 --> 00:26:08,325
Jeepers.

659
00:26:08,359 --> 00:26:10,154
-What's going on?!

660
00:26:10,189 --> 00:26:11,397
-Get behind the couch, you two.

661
00:26:11,431 --> 00:26:13,330
The redcoats are coming.

662
00:26:13,364 --> 00:26:15,159
Alright, little death satellite,

663
00:26:15,194 --> 00:26:20,061
Joely's got the exo-pinchers on,and he's nobody's sweetheart.

664
00:26:20,095 --> 00:26:22,373
You and me going
round and round,

665
00:26:22,408 --> 00:26:24,030
mano a mano.

666
00:26:24,065 --> 00:26:25,618
Here comes lunch.

667
00:26:25,653 --> 00:26:27,620
Give me the exo.

668
00:26:27,655 --> 00:26:29,622
That's right,
little doomsday machine,

669
00:26:29,657 --> 00:26:30,934
come to papa.

670
00:26:30,968 --> 00:26:34,247
Feel my steel.

671
00:26:34,282 --> 00:26:35,870
Now to bring it inside.

672
00:26:35,904 --> 00:26:38,493
Cambot,
give me the interior shot.

673
00:26:38,527 --> 00:26:40,771
-Do you have to bring
everything you find in space

674
00:26:40,806 --> 00:26:41,979
into the living room?

675
00:26:42,014 --> 00:26:43,878
-Sure looks like a
doomsday device, alright.

676
00:26:43,912 --> 00:26:45,983
Uh, Joel,
are we up a creek, here?

677
00:26:46,018 --> 00:26:47,778
-It's a doomsday machine
alright,

678
00:26:47,813 --> 00:26:49,677
and I think I actually activated

679
00:26:49,711 --> 00:26:51,920
its self-destruct mechanism.

680
00:26:51,955 --> 00:26:54,889
I'd say we have roughly an hour
to disarm this thing.

681
00:26:54,923 --> 00:26:56,062
[Machine chimes]
-Correction --

682
00:26:56,097 --> 00:26:58,513
You have an hour and 37 minutes
to disarm,

683
00:26:58,547 --> 00:27:00,549
and yes, you are up a creek.

684
00:27:00,584 --> 00:27:02,482
-Oh, golly.

685
00:27:02,517 --> 00:27:05,520
And we got commercial sign
on top of everything!

686
00:27:05,554 --> 00:27:10,076
♪♪

687
00:27:37,276 --> 00:27:39,174
[Insects chirping]

688
00:27:39,209 --> 00:27:41,521
♪♪

689
00:27:41,556 --> 00:27:43,662
-We gotta disarm that
satellite, you two.

690
00:27:43,696 --> 00:27:45,698
This is serious.

691
00:27:45,733 --> 00:27:48,183
-Going down for the count.

692
00:27:48,218 --> 00:27:49,771
♪♪

693
00:27:49,806 --> 00:27:53,119
-That was no boating accident.

694
00:27:53,154 --> 00:27:54,811
-I'm sorry I had
to kill you, Dave,

695
00:27:54,845 --> 00:27:56,502
but you understand,
I couldn't let you live.

696
00:27:56,536 --> 00:27:58,884
You see, you knew too much!

697
00:27:58,918 --> 00:28:02,025
♪♪

698
00:28:02,059 --> 00:28:04,165
-It's the Green Hornet.

699
00:28:04,199 --> 00:28:05,994
-Que tal?

700
00:28:06,029 --> 00:28:10,171
♪♪

701
00:28:10,205 --> 00:28:12,173
-[Whistles]

702
00:28:12,207 --> 00:28:20,215
♪♪

703
00:28:20,250 --> 00:28:28,258
♪♪

704
00:28:28,292 --> 00:28:29,362
-You'd think in the future,

705
00:28:29,397 --> 00:28:31,710
they'd be able
to get hip waders.

706
00:28:31,744 --> 00:28:34,782
♪♪

707
00:28:34,816 --> 00:28:36,507
-Oh, very sneaky.

708
00:28:36,542 --> 00:28:38,958
♪♪

709
00:28:38,993 --> 00:28:42,306
-He's trying to sneak
across the rice paper.

710
00:28:42,341 --> 00:28:51,799
♪♪

711
00:28:51,833 --> 00:28:56,631
-If only I could get
to my utility belt!

712
00:28:56,665 --> 00:28:58,288
Ugh!

713
00:28:58,322 --> 00:29:01,015
♪♪

714
00:29:01,049 --> 00:29:01,878
-Smooth.

715
00:29:01,912 --> 00:29:03,258
-I'll take that, Mr. Bond.

716
00:29:03,293 --> 00:29:04,259
-What are you trying to do?

717
00:29:04,294 --> 00:29:06,296
-Someone is out there.

718
00:29:06,330 --> 00:29:07,469
-Who could be out there?

719
00:29:07,504 --> 00:29:08,954
Huh?!

720
00:29:08,988 --> 00:29:11,784
♪♪

721
00:29:11,819 --> 00:29:12,923
-Take that, Mr. Bono.

722
00:29:12,958 --> 00:29:15,270
That's to you, Confucius.

723
00:29:15,305 --> 00:29:20,793
♪♪

724
00:29:20,828 --> 00:29:26,316
♪♪

725
00:29:26,350 --> 00:29:29,802
-Bring...the...car...around...

726
00:29:29,837 --> 00:29:32,046
-[Shouts indistinctly]
-...general.

727
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:33,530
-Do not take us back!

728
00:29:33,564 --> 00:29:37,396
♪♪

729
00:29:37,430 --> 00:29:40,951
-Strange way for lodge brothers
to greet each other.

730
00:29:40,986 --> 00:29:42,263
-Don't just do something!

731
00:29:42,297 --> 00:29:43,505
Stand there!

732
00:29:45,335 --> 00:29:47,544
-Oh, a neck massage.

733
00:29:47,578 --> 00:29:49,201
-Tiny...bubbles...

734
00:29:49,235 --> 00:29:51,375
in my...larynx.

735
00:29:51,410 --> 00:29:52,860
[Gagging]

736
00:29:52,894 --> 00:29:57,485
♪♪

737
00:29:57,519 --> 00:29:58,486
-I'm not on either side.

738
00:29:58,520 --> 00:30:00,315
Ooh, sorry!

739
00:30:00,350 --> 00:30:07,322
♪♪

740
00:30:07,357 --> 00:30:08,323
-Kill your brother.

741
00:30:08,358 --> 00:30:09,808
It's the only way to reinforce

742
00:30:09,842 --> 00:30:12,465
the director's
white male reality.

743
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:15,192
♪♪

744
00:30:15,227 --> 00:30:17,677
Take the shot.
Come on!

745
00:30:17,712 --> 00:30:21,992
♪♪

746
00:30:22,027 --> 00:30:23,752
-Help!

747
00:30:27,273 --> 00:30:28,861
-Mom...always...

748
00:30:28,896 --> 00:30:31,243
did like you...best.

749
00:30:31,277 --> 00:30:33,935
♪♪

750
00:30:33,970 --> 00:30:35,592
-Gee, I --

751
00:30:35,626 --> 00:30:37,076
I missed.

752
00:30:37,111 --> 00:30:43,255
♪♪

753
00:30:43,289 --> 00:30:45,395
-Well, I know you just
shot your brother,

754
00:30:45,429 --> 00:30:47,915
but why don't you
just come over here

755
00:30:47,949 --> 00:30:50,227
and love me a little bit.

756
00:30:50,262 --> 00:30:52,885
Yeah.

757
00:30:52,920 --> 00:30:53,955
-[Sobbing]

758
00:30:56,268 --> 00:30:57,441
Love me.

759
00:30:57,476 --> 00:30:58,926
Love me!

760
00:30:58,960 --> 00:31:02,412
♪♪

761
00:31:02,446 --> 00:31:05,622
-Meanwhile at the ocarina.

762
00:31:05,656 --> 00:31:08,383
-10,000 miles, sir.

763
00:31:08,418 --> 00:31:10,213
Velocity now 5 miles per second.

764
00:31:10,247 --> 00:31:11,628
-Alright, Allen.

765
00:31:11,662 --> 00:31:14,355
Set gravitation compensators
for an altitude of 50 miles.

766
00:31:14,389 --> 00:31:16,253
Decelerate to zero velocity.

767
00:31:16,288 --> 00:31:17,910
-GC 50 miles.

768
00:31:17,945 --> 00:31:19,739
Zero velocity.

769
00:31:19,774 --> 00:31:24,330
♪♪

770
00:31:24,365 --> 00:31:25,884
-500.
-500 what?

771
00:31:25,918 --> 00:31:27,609
-Stand by emergency.

772
00:31:29,577 --> 00:31:31,199
-300.
-What?!

773
00:31:31,234 --> 00:31:32,304
-200.

774
00:31:32,338 --> 00:31:33,857
-Stop teasing me.

775
00:31:33,892 --> 00:31:36,584
-150.

776
00:31:36,618 --> 00:31:38,034
100.
-Sold.

777
00:31:38,068 --> 00:31:39,345
-75.

778
00:31:39,380 --> 00:31:40,588
60.

779
00:31:40,622 --> 00:31:42,314
55.

780
00:31:42,348 --> 00:31:43,073
50.

781
00:31:43,108 --> 00:31:44,281
-And so on and so on.

782
00:31:44,316 --> 00:31:46,559
Stationary orbit, sir.

783
00:31:46,594 --> 00:31:48,458
-50 miles and locked in, sir.

784
00:31:48,492 --> 00:31:49,631
-Very good, Mr. Scott.

785
00:31:49,666 --> 00:31:52,669
-Told you he'd like it.

786
00:31:52,703 --> 00:31:54,153
-Oh, picture, picture.

787
00:31:54,188 --> 00:31:57,916
-...would you join me on
the observation deck, please.

788
00:31:57,950 --> 00:31:59,641
-He asked me!
He asked me!

789
00:32:01,781 --> 00:32:03,128
-Just let me slip
my dance belt on.

790
00:32:03,162 --> 00:32:04,715
-So, we've finally arrived.

791
00:32:04,750 --> 00:32:07,028
-Did you have any doubt,
Captain?

792
00:32:07,063 --> 00:32:09,410
-About reaching Solaris? No.

793
00:32:09,444 --> 00:32:11,412
-Let's see.
Looks like a hairline fracture.

794
00:32:11,446 --> 00:32:14,070
-...still alive, Dr. Farrell?

795
00:32:14,104 --> 00:32:15,968
-Well, there's always a chance.

796
00:32:16,003 --> 00:32:18,246
-Not a very good chance,
I believe.

797
00:32:18,281 --> 00:32:19,938
-Oh, what do you know?

798
00:32:19,972 --> 00:32:22,768
-[Sighs] Yes, the facts
do indicate that this planet

799
00:32:22,802 --> 00:32:26,599
has an unpleasant environment,
but not necessarily hostile.

800
00:32:26,634 --> 00:32:29,430
It has a carbon-water
oxygen system.

801
00:32:29,464 --> 00:32:31,742
-Sounds kinda like a Slurpee.

802
00:32:31,777 --> 00:32:33,261
-[Speaks native language]

803
00:32:33,296 --> 00:32:34,642
-Huh?

804
00:32:34,676 --> 00:32:36,816
-Jung has calculated that

805
00:32:36,851 --> 00:32:39,509
for the past three months
while we've been traveling,

806
00:32:39,543 --> 00:32:43,685
18 years have gone by
on the planet.

807
00:32:43,720 --> 00:32:45,998
-That means it'll be legal
to date my girlfriend now.

808
00:32:46,033 --> 00:32:49,139
My CD should be rolling over
just about now.

809
00:32:49,174 --> 00:32:51,417
-I believe our admiral
will not give up

810
00:32:51,452 --> 00:32:52,867
until he has definitely proved

811
00:32:52,901 --> 00:32:54,351
there are no survivors.

812
00:32:54,386 --> 00:32:55,456
-He's gonna get sleeves someday.

813
00:32:55,490 --> 00:32:57,872
-It'll probably
cost him his command.

814
00:32:59,598 --> 00:33:01,496
-Once, there was such leaders

815
00:33:01,531 --> 00:33:03,464
on Centaurus.

816
00:33:03,498 --> 00:33:06,053
-The admiral has his way,
there will be again.

817
00:33:06,087 --> 00:33:07,123
He's determined to prove

818
00:33:07,157 --> 00:33:09,090
that Centaurus
can rebuild its culture.

819
00:33:09,125 --> 00:33:10,747
-What a kiss-up.

820
00:33:10,781 --> 00:33:13,577
-...this is so, Doctor?

821
00:33:13,612 --> 00:33:15,027
-Well, I'd say that Linda

822
00:33:15,062 --> 00:33:17,478
is a brilliant example
of what can be done.

823
00:33:17,512 --> 00:33:19,825
-Through bizarre
experimentation.

824
00:33:19,859 --> 00:33:22,379
-When she was removed
to the colony,

825
00:33:22,414 --> 00:33:24,692
the others were not.

826
00:33:24,726 --> 00:33:26,797
-What are you trying
to tell me, Jung?

827
00:33:26,832 --> 00:33:30,042
-I abhor you, round eye,
and all your ways.

828
00:33:30,077 --> 00:33:32,493
-...a people will
change their ways

829
00:33:32,527 --> 00:33:36,324
only if they wish to do so.

830
00:33:36,359 --> 00:33:38,533
A man may be removed from...

831
00:33:38,568 --> 00:33:40,570
-Uh, wait, wait.
Let me get a pencil here.

832
00:33:40,604 --> 00:33:44,436
-But it is difficult to
remove the land from the man.

833
00:33:44,470 --> 00:33:47,059
-All: And you can't remove
the land from the man,

834
00:33:47,094 --> 00:33:51,167
the chalice from the palace,
and the brew that is true.

835
00:33:51,201 --> 00:33:54,066
-The flagon with the dragon
has the pellet with the poison.

836
00:33:54,101 --> 00:33:55,585
♪♪

837
00:33:55,619 --> 00:33:57,863
-It's such a beautiful planet.

838
00:33:57,897 --> 00:34:00,452
-Hey, settle down.
It's just a painting.

839
00:34:00,486 --> 00:34:02,488
-...Centaurus.

840
00:34:02,523 --> 00:34:04,214
So green and peaceful.

841
00:34:04,249 --> 00:34:06,975
-It may not be so peaceful
down there.

842
00:34:07,010 --> 00:34:08,287
-Yeah, the grass
is always greener

843
00:34:08,322 --> 00:34:11,325
on the other side of the galaxy.

844
00:34:11,359 --> 00:34:13,189
-We can't trust young planets.

845
00:34:13,223 --> 00:34:14,707
-What do you mean, Admiral?

846
00:34:14,742 --> 00:34:16,813
-They're lazy,
and they listen to loud music.

847
00:34:16,847 --> 00:34:19,609
-...living things
completely unknown to us.

848
00:34:19,643 --> 00:34:22,336
-Hasn't this planet
ever been explored?

849
00:34:22,370 --> 00:34:24,476
-Not according to
our galactic records.

850
00:34:24,510 --> 00:34:25,891
-But, uh...
then I couldn't read 'em,

851
00:34:25,925 --> 00:34:27,341
'cause I spilled coffee on 'em.

852
00:34:27,375 --> 00:34:28,997
-No sign of life of any kind,
Admiral --

853
00:34:29,032 --> 00:34:31,483
human or otherwise.

854
00:34:31,517 --> 00:34:35,073
-The creatures on these planets
are deceiving.

855
00:34:35,107 --> 00:34:36,108
Feet on the ground,

856
00:34:36,143 --> 00:34:39,180
you can't always trust
what you see.

857
00:34:39,215 --> 00:34:42,080
-Bill, how do you
fight your panty lines?

858
00:34:42,114 --> 00:34:44,151
-Huh? Oh, that's man talk.

859
00:34:44,185 --> 00:34:45,462
We'll never understand it.

860
00:34:45,497 --> 00:34:47,809
-18 years is a long time.

861
00:34:47,844 --> 00:34:49,535
-And they couldn't have
picked a worse place to crash.

862
00:34:49,570 --> 00:34:53,574
-Hey says that like
there's a good place to crash.

863
00:34:53,608 --> 00:34:55,921
-Certainly
a botanist's paradise.

864
00:34:55,955 --> 00:34:57,129
-I hate to tell you this, Phil,

865
00:34:57,164 --> 00:34:59,235
but all your aquarium fish
are dead.

866
00:34:59,269 --> 00:35:03,308
-...comparable to our bison
about 5 million years ago.

867
00:35:03,342 --> 00:35:05,068
-Doc, uh...

868
00:35:06,794 --> 00:35:09,590
...could man exist...

869
00:35:09,624 --> 00:35:12,110
in that environment?

870
00:35:12,144 --> 00:35:13,732
-Probably.

871
00:35:13,766 --> 00:35:15,251
-Let's all speak in...

872
00:35:15,285 --> 00:35:17,011
broken sentences.

873
00:35:17,045 --> 00:35:20,290
-I think it would be mostly
combating the elements,

874
00:35:20,325 --> 00:35:22,603
defending himself from...

875
00:35:22,637 --> 00:35:24,432
who knows what.

876
00:35:24,467 --> 00:35:26,641
There could be some
rather ferocious beasts

877
00:35:26,676 --> 00:35:28,367
roaming about that terrain.

878
00:35:28,402 --> 00:35:31,198
-Are the scenes just as static
down on the planet, Bill?

879
00:35:31,232 --> 00:35:34,028
-...to Command Headquarters.

880
00:35:34,062 --> 00:35:36,962
Inform them we're going to
make a landing on this planet.

881
00:35:36,996 --> 00:35:39,240
-And use the good stationery.

882
00:35:39,275 --> 00:35:40,448
-Yep.
-That deep canyon.

883
00:35:40,483 --> 00:35:42,209
-Uh-huh.
-Beyond the jungle.

884
00:35:42,243 --> 00:35:44,176
-Mm-hmm.
-Yeah, I see it.

885
00:35:44,211 --> 00:35:45,281
-That's your landing site.

886
00:35:45,315 --> 00:35:46,592
-Oh.
-You mean it?

887
00:35:46,627 --> 00:35:47,869
-Sergeant Allen.

888
00:35:47,904 --> 00:35:49,043
-Yep.
-Landing stations.

889
00:35:49,077 --> 00:35:50,700
-Gotcha.
-Yes, sir.

890
00:35:50,734 --> 00:35:52,046
-Who should I tell
these orders to?

891
00:35:52,080 --> 00:35:53,668
-Attention all personnel,
attention all personnel --

892
00:35:53,703 --> 00:35:56,361
landing stations.

893
00:35:56,395 --> 00:35:58,121
-Stand by gravitators.

894
00:35:58,156 --> 00:35:59,847
-Maneuvering room,
stand by gravitators.

895
00:35:59,881 --> 00:36:01,089
-Gotcha.

896
00:36:01,124 --> 00:36:03,368
-Maneuvering room ready
and standing by.

897
00:36:03,402 --> 00:36:05,887
-Thanks, angel.
-Hate to say it, Lieutenant,

898
00:36:05,922 --> 00:36:07,751
but I got a feeling
the old man's got

899
00:36:07,786 --> 00:36:09,926
a bad case of cosmic fever.

900
00:36:09,960 --> 00:36:11,514
-Well, I've got some salve
in my foot locker.

901
00:36:11,548 --> 00:36:12,653
-...spirit of adventure, Chief?

902
00:36:12,687 --> 00:36:13,930
I thought you loved this life.

903
00:36:13,964 --> 00:36:16,450
-Spirit I've got,
adventure I've had,

904
00:36:16,484 --> 00:36:18,866
love I was looking forward to.

905
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:21,248
-I know what you mean, Chief.

906
00:36:21,282 --> 00:36:23,284
Remember the words of that
great planetary prophet.

907
00:36:23,319 --> 00:36:24,423
-Yeah, what'd he say?

908
00:36:24,458 --> 00:36:25,700
-He said, "Let them
that don't want none

909
00:36:25,735 --> 00:36:27,357
have memories of..."

910
00:36:27,392 --> 00:36:28,289
Forget it.

911
00:36:28,324 --> 00:36:29,842
-[Laughs]
-Great proverb.

912
00:36:29,877 --> 00:36:33,329
Hope you didn't get any of that
tattooed anyplace important.

913
00:36:33,363 --> 00:36:35,089
-Bzzz!

914
00:36:35,123 --> 00:36:37,298
-Hey, I see a spot.
Let's pull up over there.

915
00:36:39,093 --> 00:36:40,232
-Bzzz.

916
00:36:40,267 --> 00:36:44,754
♪♪

917
00:37:11,953 --> 00:37:14,232
-Alright, let's take a break.

918
00:37:14,266 --> 00:37:15,405
Go easy on the water.

919
00:37:15,440 --> 00:37:17,649
-It eliminates rest stops.

920
00:37:19,720 --> 00:37:22,205
-Everyone take one
oxygenian tablet.

921
00:37:22,240 --> 00:37:23,758
No more.

922
00:37:23,793 --> 00:37:26,071
-I didn't say Simon Says.

923
00:37:26,105 --> 00:37:27,728
-Like breathing under water.

924
00:37:27,762 --> 00:37:29,350
-Hot water.
-We'll get used to it.

925
00:37:29,385 --> 00:37:30,800
-It's like being
in the crews' quarters.

926
00:37:30,834 --> 00:37:33,389
-Imagine having to live
in an inferno like this.

927
00:37:33,423 --> 00:37:36,219
-Probably not too bad
up in the highlands.

928
00:37:36,254 --> 00:37:38,635
-This planet's not
too unlike Centauria.

929
00:37:38,670 --> 00:37:41,949
-Aside from the fact that
it's completely different.

930
00:37:41,983 --> 00:37:43,295
-I imagine the Centaurians could

931
00:37:43,330 --> 00:37:45,159
adapt to this planet
very easily.

932
00:37:45,193 --> 00:37:46,988
-With all this vegetation?

933
00:37:47,023 --> 00:37:49,853
Naw, they like the deserts, Doc.

934
00:37:49,888 --> 00:37:52,200
You give them barren,
windswept oceans of sand.

935
00:37:52,235 --> 00:37:53,478
-All: Shut up!

936
00:37:53,512 --> 00:37:54,513
-Say, Doc...

937
00:37:54,548 --> 00:37:55,859
-Do you think
I'll be offed first?

938
00:37:55,894 --> 00:37:58,241
-Uh, I mean Mr. Farrell.

939
00:37:58,276 --> 00:38:00,208
Old Jung said that
in the three months

940
00:38:00,243 --> 00:38:02,280
it took us
to get back to this planet,

941
00:38:02,314 --> 00:38:05,386
18 years passed by here.

942
00:38:05,421 --> 00:38:06,732
How does he figure that?

943
00:38:06,767 --> 00:38:09,390
-He was kidding.-...told you half a dozen times.

944
00:38:09,425 --> 00:38:12,186
It's the time paradox.
What's a'matter with you?

945
00:38:12,220 --> 00:38:14,361
-O-Oh, yeah, I, uh --
I missed that day.

946
00:38:14,395 --> 00:38:16,259
Sorry.
-...I still don't see it.

947
00:38:16,294 --> 00:38:17,502
-It's a matter of reference...

948
00:38:17,536 --> 00:38:19,435
-Uh, it's too complicated
for you, bonehead.

949
00:38:19,469 --> 00:38:20,919
-...traveling at one speed.

950
00:38:20,953 --> 00:38:22,886
This planet travels at another.

951
00:38:22,921 --> 00:38:24,129
-You get it, kid?

952
00:38:24,163 --> 00:38:26,062
For three months,
we've been barreling along

953
00:38:26,096 --> 00:38:28,616
at nearly speed of light.

954
00:38:28,651 --> 00:38:30,791
-Yeah, yeah.
I-I know that.

955
00:38:30,825 --> 00:38:32,620
But I-I --
-Look. Look.

956
00:38:32,655 --> 00:38:37,556
This planet rotates on its axis
at only 1,000 miles an hour.

957
00:38:37,591 --> 00:38:40,663
Now, that speed determines
the length of the day,

958
00:38:40,697 --> 00:38:43,355
the month, and the year here.
-We getting through?

959
00:38:43,390 --> 00:38:44,460
-Why don't you come up
to my room tonight,

960
00:38:44,494 --> 00:38:47,083
and I'll explain it to ya.

961
00:38:47,117 --> 00:38:49,879
-Oh, look, uh, at the speed
we were traveling in space

962
00:38:49,913 --> 00:38:52,364
for three months,
that was the equivalent

963
00:38:52,399 --> 00:38:54,780
of six thousand, five hundred --

964
00:38:54,815 --> 00:38:57,783
-La, la, la, la,
la, la, la, la, la!

965
00:38:57,818 --> 00:38:59,233
Can't hear you!

966
00:38:59,267 --> 00:39:01,373
-La, la, la, la!

967
00:39:01,408 --> 00:39:02,892
-Yeah, sure.

968
00:39:02,926 --> 00:39:04,859
It's simple.
It's, uh --

969
00:39:04,894 --> 00:39:07,241
It's just a matter
of reference points.

970
00:39:07,275 --> 00:39:08,415
Anybody can see that.

971
00:39:08,449 --> 00:39:11,072
-He's bluffing.

972
00:39:11,107 --> 00:39:12,557
-He's a twit.

973
00:39:12,591 --> 00:39:13,868
-See anything?

974
00:39:13,903 --> 00:39:15,249
-A few birds.

975
00:39:15,283 --> 00:39:17,389
Nothing else.

976
00:39:17,424 --> 00:39:19,460
-What'd he expect to see,
a welcoming committee?

977
00:39:19,495 --> 00:39:20,772
-Don't get funny.

978
00:39:20,806 --> 00:39:22,601
-Don't worry.
He won't.

979
00:39:22,636 --> 00:39:24,431
-Hey.

980
00:39:24,465 --> 00:39:25,880
You hear something, Chief?

981
00:39:25,915 --> 00:39:28,400
-I don't think
it's a welcoming committee.

982
00:39:28,435 --> 00:39:30,022
-Like something walking around
over there.

983
00:39:30,057 --> 00:39:32,300
-Something big.

984
00:39:32,335 --> 00:39:33,681
-[Growls]

985
00:39:33,716 --> 00:39:36,615
-Don't anybody move.

986
00:39:36,650 --> 00:39:38,686
-Oh, my God.

987
00:39:38,721 --> 00:39:40,792
-It's an insert shot
of an iguana!

988
00:39:40,826 --> 00:39:42,966
-Their technology must be
light years ahead of ours!

989
00:39:43,001 --> 00:39:45,313
Their use of stock footage
is amazing!

990
00:39:45,348 --> 00:39:47,143
♪♪

991
00:39:47,177 --> 00:39:48,144
-Oh, great.

992
00:39:48,178 --> 00:39:49,387
That's your answer
to everything.

993
00:39:49,421 --> 00:39:51,216
-How do you like
your lizard done, boys?

994
00:39:51,250 --> 00:39:53,425
-I never knew lizards
were so darn flammable.

995
00:39:53,460 --> 00:39:56,739
-No thunder lizards were hurtduring the filming of this film.

996
00:39:56,773 --> 00:39:59,224
-Mmm! Extra "cripsy."

997
00:39:59,258 --> 00:40:01,882
-"Cripsy?"
-Yeah.

998
00:40:01,916 --> 00:40:04,194
-You alright, Allen?
-Sure. Can I go change now?

999
00:40:04,229 --> 00:40:06,093
-Yeah, yeah.
I think --

1000
00:40:06,127 --> 00:40:08,095
-I soiled myself.

1001
00:40:08,129 --> 00:40:09,165
-What is it, Allen?

1002
00:40:09,199 --> 00:40:10,856
-I c--

1003
00:40:10,891 --> 00:40:12,410
I can't find my heartbeat!
-[Laughs]

1004
00:40:12,444 --> 00:40:14,584
Nothing like a little adventure,eh, Lieutenant?

1005
00:40:14,619 --> 00:40:16,897
-Try lower.
-Set up a beamer here.

1006
00:40:16,931 --> 00:40:18,450
-BMW?

1007
00:40:23,386 --> 00:40:25,457
-Cosmos I,this is Scott.
Do you receive me?

1008
00:40:25,492 --> 00:40:27,977
-Gutless 1,we read you.
-...receiving you, Commander.

1009
00:40:28,011 --> 00:40:29,841
Stevens standing by.

1010
00:40:29,875 --> 00:40:32,291
-Ensign, inform the admiral
that I advise no one be allowed

1011
00:40:32,326 --> 00:40:35,225
to leave the ship
without being well armed.

1012
00:40:35,260 --> 00:40:36,364
-Scott, this is King.

1013
00:40:36,399 --> 00:40:38,470
-Are you speaking
into a Waterpik?

1014
00:40:38,505 --> 00:40:40,472
-Well, we've encountered one
of those deceptive creatures

1015
00:40:40,507 --> 00:40:41,784
you mentioned.

1016
00:40:41,818 --> 00:40:43,095
A giant lizard.

1017
00:40:43,130 --> 00:40:44,269
It's been destroyed,

1018
00:40:44,303 --> 00:40:46,892
but I'm sure
there are others in this area.

1019
00:40:46,927 --> 00:40:49,205
We're about halfway to
the crash site now, Admiral.

1020
00:40:49,239 --> 00:40:51,310
I'll leave a beamer
at this location.

1021
00:40:51,345 --> 00:40:52,450
That's all, sir.

1022
00:40:52,484 --> 00:40:53,934
-Very well, Mr. Scott.

1023
00:40:53,968 --> 00:40:55,970
Carry on.
Keep a sharp watch.

1024
00:40:56,005 --> 00:40:58,594
Don't want to be caught
with your guard down.

1025
00:40:58,628 --> 00:41:01,182
-Yes, sir.

1026
00:41:01,217 --> 00:41:02,494
-"Very well, Mr. Scott.

1027
00:41:02,529 --> 00:41:04,634
Carry on.
Keep a sharp watch.

1028
00:41:04,669 --> 00:41:07,016
You don't want to be caught
with your guard down."

1029
00:41:07,050 --> 00:41:09,052
-Stop doing a Cagney impression.

1030
00:41:09,087 --> 00:41:10,951
It's not that great.

1031
00:41:10,985 --> 00:41:13,056
-My commanding officer
asked for volunteers

1032
00:41:13,091 --> 00:41:15,231
for an important expedition.

1033
00:41:15,265 --> 00:41:16,922
I thought he meant exhibition.

1034
00:41:16,957 --> 00:41:19,615
-Yeah, and my uncle's
the producer in the film, too.

1035
00:41:19,649 --> 00:41:21,340
-Alright, men, let's move.

1036
00:41:21,375 --> 00:41:24,792
And keep a sharp watch.

1037
00:41:24,827 --> 00:41:26,691
-It's our first Christmas
on the planet,

1038
00:41:26,725 --> 00:41:28,451
and you've been just grand.

1039
00:41:28,486 --> 00:41:30,453
♪♪

1040
00:41:30,488 --> 00:41:31,765
We better go be
with the others now.

1041
00:41:31,799 --> 00:41:33,594
-If that the way
they grow lizards around here,

1042
00:41:33,629 --> 00:41:35,769
I'd hate to run into a snake.

1043
00:41:35,803 --> 00:41:37,115
-[Chuckles]

1044
00:41:37,149 --> 00:41:38,530
-Good one.

1045
00:41:38,565 --> 00:41:40,636
-There's a well on this ship.

1046
00:41:40,670 --> 00:41:41,740
-What?!

1047
00:41:41,775 --> 00:41:43,639
-Sorry, men,
your liberty's canceled.

1048
00:41:43,673 --> 00:41:45,986
No one is to leave this ship
under any circumstances.

1049
00:41:46,020 --> 00:41:48,057
-Have they located
the Cosmos III,sir?

1050
00:41:48,091 --> 00:41:49,437
-Not yet.

1051
00:41:49,472 --> 00:41:51,336
-Ah, well, there goes
the ball game, huh?

1052
00:41:51,370 --> 00:41:53,441
-They must've run
into trouble out there.

1053
00:41:53,476 --> 00:41:54,650
-Well, I wish I was with them.

1054
00:41:54,684 --> 00:41:56,168
Anything'd be better
than being stuck in this

1055
00:41:56,203 --> 00:41:57,653
king-sized community capsule.

1056
00:41:57,687 --> 00:41:59,758
-Oh, you...
-Admiral King.

1057
00:41:59,793 --> 00:42:01,519
Linda is out of the ship.

1058
00:42:01,553 --> 00:42:02,658
-Are you sure?

1059
00:42:02,692 --> 00:42:05,592
-She said she wished
to breathe the fresh air,

1060
00:42:05,626 --> 00:42:08,008
to feel the warm of the sun.

1061
00:42:08,042 --> 00:42:10,148
-Just tell me where she went,
Hemingway, come on.

1062
00:42:10,182 --> 00:42:11,908
-...your permission.

1063
00:42:11,943 --> 00:42:16,223
-She's been dying to get off
this ship ever since we landed.

1064
00:42:16,257 --> 00:42:19,226
-Will it help
if I strike a pose right here?

1065
00:42:19,260 --> 00:42:23,333
♪♪

1066
00:42:23,368 --> 00:42:24,887
-Harris, get my field gear.

1067
00:42:24,921 --> 00:42:26,095
-And my stirrup pants.

1068
00:42:26,129 --> 00:42:28,097
-Tell him that we'll be --
-Admiral King.

1069
00:42:28,131 --> 00:42:29,961
You can't take this chance, sir.

1070
00:42:29,995 --> 00:42:32,515
If anything should happen to youor -- or Commander Scott...

1071
00:42:32,550 --> 00:42:34,068
-Yes, yes, you'd be in charge.

1072
00:42:34,103 --> 00:42:36,692
Yes, I realize that.

1073
00:42:36,726 --> 00:42:38,763
-Harris,
you and Wilson go look for her.

1074
00:42:38,797 --> 00:42:40,247
She's probably near the ship.

1075
00:42:40,281 --> 00:42:42,698
-Yes, sir.
-Bring laser rifles with you.

1076
00:42:42,732 --> 00:42:44,838
-I'll stay here
and take more lithium.

1077
00:42:44,872 --> 00:42:46,702
-Yes, sir.
-Don't worry, Admiral.

1078
00:42:46,736 --> 00:42:50,429
We'll find her.
-Spin and Marty to the rescue!

1079
00:42:50,464 --> 00:42:51,914
-Poor Linda.

1080
00:42:51,948 --> 00:42:55,262
She's such a lost child.

1081
00:42:55,296 --> 00:42:56,953
-Karen...

1082
00:42:56,988 --> 00:42:59,093
anything happens to her, I...

1083
00:42:59,128 --> 00:43:01,579
I'll never forgive myself.

1084
00:43:01,613 --> 00:43:03,373
-I understand.

1085
00:43:03,408 --> 00:43:05,030
-She's just like her mother.
-Oh, Ward.

1086
00:43:05,065 --> 00:43:06,653
The beaver turned out okay.

1087
00:43:06,687 --> 00:43:07,895
-...curious,

1088
00:43:07,930 --> 00:43:11,381
filled with childlike dreams
of another world.

1089
00:43:11,416 --> 00:43:12,762
-What is she,
Anne of Green Gables,

1090
00:43:12,797 --> 00:43:13,660
all the sudden?

1091
00:43:13,694 --> 00:43:15,731
Hey!
-...cannot be changed.

1092
00:43:20,356 --> 00:43:22,013
-What did he want?

1093
00:43:22,047 --> 00:43:23,635
-Oops.

1094
00:43:23,670 --> 00:43:26,155
-I didn't know
he knew Linda's mother.

1095
00:43:26,189 --> 00:43:29,054
I thought she was killed
in the Centaurians travel war.

1096
00:43:29,089 --> 00:43:30,469
-It is true,

1097
00:43:30,504 --> 00:43:33,887
but Admiral knew her
long before that.

1098
00:43:33,921 --> 00:43:35,543
He made many expedition

1099
00:43:35,578 --> 00:43:37,994
into the desert in those years.

1100
00:43:38,029 --> 00:43:39,202
-Ah, wait a minute.
Wait a minute.

1101
00:43:39,237 --> 00:43:40,618
You're describing
the plot of "Kung Fu."

1102
00:43:40,652 --> 00:43:41,929
-Who was Linda's father?

1103
00:43:41,964 --> 00:43:43,241
-We better get outta here.
-Yeah, you're right.

1104
00:43:43,275 --> 00:43:45,174
-Oh, boy.-We gotta disarm that satellite!

1105
00:43:45,208 --> 00:43:47,728
Oh, yeah, I want to help!
-Let's get going.

1106
00:43:51,145 --> 00:43:55,425
♪♪

1107
00:44:22,280 --> 00:44:24,213
-You know,
this makes no sense at all.

1108
00:44:24,247 --> 00:44:26,008
Whoever heard of
a doomsday machine

1109
00:44:26,042 --> 00:44:29,425
with a fold-lock top in it.
-Hey, hey, Joel. Look.

1110
00:44:29,459 --> 00:44:31,427
I found an instruction manual
right there, see.

1111
00:44:31,461 --> 00:44:32,531
-Thanks.

1112
00:44:32,566 --> 00:44:34,223
It says, "Isaac Asimov's

1113
00:44:34,257 --> 00:44:36,397
Literary Doomsday Machine."

1114
00:44:36,432 --> 00:44:37,847
It's an instruction manual.

1115
00:44:37,882 --> 00:44:40,160
It must be over
1,000 pages long.

1116
00:44:40,194 --> 00:44:42,680
How typical.
-Literary Doomsday?

1117
00:44:42,714 --> 00:44:44,095
Isn't that when
your library fines

1118
00:44:44,129 --> 00:44:45,683
exceed the price of the book?

1119
00:44:45,717 --> 00:44:48,133
-Uh, I thought it was, uh...
every time Jackie Collins

1120
00:44:48,168 --> 00:44:49,479
makes it
to the best-seller list.

1121
00:44:49,514 --> 00:44:51,102
-Ah, settle down, you goonheads.

1122
00:44:51,136 --> 00:44:54,070
We got this doomsday machine
to disarm and, well,

1123
00:44:54,105 --> 00:44:55,278
we gotta check the manual.

1124
00:44:55,313 --> 00:44:56,314
Let's see.

1125
00:44:56,348 --> 00:44:57,798
It looks like it's translated

1126
00:44:57,833 --> 00:44:59,489
from the Korean or something.

1127
00:44:59,524 --> 00:45:01,595
-They must have, uh...
subcontracted the satellite

1128
00:45:01,629 --> 00:45:03,528
for 'em.
-Uh, I don't --

1129
00:45:03,562 --> 00:45:05,633
I don't get it.
Asimov must've gone mad.

1130
00:45:05,668 --> 00:45:07,739
Why would anyone want
to make a doomsday machine?

1131
00:45:07,774 --> 00:45:08,947
-Well, he's probably mad

1132
00:45:08,982 --> 00:45:10,638
'cause no one else
is as smart as him,

1133
00:45:10,673 --> 00:45:13,434
or else that L. Ron Hubbard
has more followers than him.

1134
00:45:13,469 --> 00:45:15,229
-Well, didn't Asimov
try to establish

1135
00:45:15,264 --> 00:45:17,093
the Church of the Super Quiz
once?

1136
00:45:17,128 --> 00:45:18,785
-Oh, knock it off,
you spinach shins.

1137
00:45:18,819 --> 00:45:20,545
I gotta read this manual.

1138
00:45:20,579 --> 00:45:21,788
"Step one --

1139
00:45:21,822 --> 00:45:25,101
It will be very enjoyable
for you to separate

1140
00:45:25,136 --> 00:45:27,655
the ocular filter coupling

1141
00:45:27,690 --> 00:45:30,313
of from the decapaciter,

1142
00:45:30,348 --> 00:45:31,763
which is stout

1143
00:45:31,798 --> 00:45:34,179
and yellow sometimes."

1144
00:45:34,214 --> 00:45:36,872
Crow, you better scan this
and give me the instructions.

1145
00:45:36,906 --> 00:45:37,907
-[Whirring]

1146
00:45:37,942 --> 00:45:39,944
Got it.
-Brother.

1147
00:45:39,978 --> 00:45:40,738
Okay.

1148
00:45:40,772 --> 00:45:42,981
"Most very kindly, uh...

1149
00:45:43,016 --> 00:45:44,776
find the lookee switch,
which is nice

1150
00:45:44,811 --> 00:45:46,433
and sitting there
with green label

1151
00:45:46,467 --> 00:45:48,435
that leaves you singing."

1152
00:45:48,469 --> 00:45:51,887
-I think I got it.
-Okay.

1153
00:45:51,921 --> 00:45:53,026
"Carefully disregard

1154
00:45:53,060 --> 00:45:54,682
and do not do
the very wrong thing

1155
00:45:54,717 --> 00:45:56,615
or much confusion
will result..."

1156
00:45:56,650 --> 00:45:57,513
Tell me about it.

1157
00:45:57,547 --> 00:45:58,790
"...with sparks, flowers,

1158
00:45:58,825 --> 00:46:01,379
and loud report on some models.

1159
00:46:01,413 --> 00:46:03,243
Glue Batman to CG --

1160
00:46:03,277 --> 00:46:05,314
detail omitted for clarity?"

1161
00:46:05,348 --> 00:46:07,212
This is really confusing.

1162
00:46:07,247 --> 00:46:11,354
-Who wrote this --
Charlie Callas?

1163
00:46:11,389 --> 00:46:12,839
-Hey, oh, oh.
Wait, there's more.

1164
00:46:12,873 --> 00:46:14,185
Uh, uh, it says, uh...

1165
00:46:14,219 --> 00:46:17,015
"Clip, uh, red wire
likes you best

1166
00:46:17,050 --> 00:46:19,535
with firm hand and glad heart."

1167
00:46:19,569 --> 00:46:22,365
-Okay.
I think that outta do it.

1168
00:46:22,400 --> 00:46:25,368
-"But first...
clip the blue wire.

1169
00:46:25,403 --> 00:46:27,094
Got you, scrawny man."
-[Laughs]

1170
00:46:27,129 --> 00:46:29,441
[Alarm blaring]
Well, that's what it said.

1171
00:46:29,476 --> 00:46:31,271
-Oh, man, we got movies.

1172
00:46:31,305 --> 00:46:32,997
[All screaming]

1173
00:46:36,276 --> 00:46:38,899
[Whirring]

1174
00:46:38,934 --> 00:46:40,487
[Door creaks]

1175
00:46:41,902 --> 00:46:44,215
♪♪

1176
00:46:44,249 --> 00:46:47,114
-Oh, my eyes.

1177
00:46:47,149 --> 00:46:49,945
-You got creamed.

1178
00:46:49,979 --> 00:46:51,809
You okay?

1179
00:46:51,843 --> 00:46:58,091
♪♪

1180
00:46:58,125 --> 00:46:59,333
-Say...
-[Screams]

1181
00:46:59,368 --> 00:47:01,957
-Seems like a nice lookin' gal!

1182
00:47:01,991 --> 00:47:05,063
-Are you chaffing like a bear?
Or is it just me?

1183
00:47:05,098 --> 00:47:06,513
-It sounded like
a bird call to me.

1184
00:47:06,547 --> 00:47:07,928
-Sounded like a girl
screaming at a snake.

1185
00:47:07,963 --> 00:47:09,447
-Come on.
Linda's in trouble.

1186
00:47:09,481 --> 00:47:11,518
♪♪

1187
00:47:11,552 --> 00:47:15,453
-Hey, the snake pushed
her into the river.

1188
00:47:15,487 --> 00:47:18,421
-Fortunately, I have
my Johnny Rocket crossbow.

1189
00:47:18,456 --> 00:47:20,423
♪♪

1190
00:47:20,458 --> 00:47:21,908
-Snake on a stick.

1191
00:47:21,942 --> 00:47:24,772
♪♪

1192
00:47:24,807 --> 00:47:26,429
-Hey, look what
someone threw back.

1193
00:47:26,464 --> 00:47:28,535
Cool. This'll look great
over the mantle.

1194
00:47:28,569 --> 00:47:30,709
I can't believe it.
This is a keeper.

1195
00:47:30,744 --> 00:47:33,851
♪♪

1196
00:47:33,885 --> 00:47:36,129
-Hey, watch those hands.

1197
00:47:36,163 --> 00:47:38,303
-Amazing what you find
if you just look.

1198
00:47:38,338 --> 00:47:40,996
♪♪

1199
00:47:41,030 --> 00:47:43,101
-She'll be perfect
for my experiments.

1200
00:47:43,136 --> 00:47:45,621
-And Quincey will
be so proud of me.

1201
00:47:45,655 --> 00:47:48,658
♪♪

1202
00:47:48,693 --> 00:47:50,695
-Say...

1203
00:47:50,729 --> 00:47:53,111
this hotel's got everything.

1204
00:47:53,146 --> 00:47:54,941
Wonder where the game room is.

1205
00:47:54,975 --> 00:48:04,502
♪♪

1206
00:48:04,536 --> 00:48:07,332
-Ah.

1207
00:48:07,367 --> 00:48:09,541
Get a load of this place,
will ya.

1208
00:48:09,576 --> 00:48:11,509
It's a regular paradise island.

1209
00:48:11,543 --> 00:48:13,856
-I hear you, man.

1210
00:48:13,891 --> 00:48:15,858
-It's also the end of the trail,

1211
00:48:15,893 --> 00:48:17,929
unless you're a mountain goat.

1212
00:48:17,964 --> 00:48:19,068
-Oh, I used to be.

1213
00:48:19,103 --> 00:48:20,414
-...she climbed up there.

1214
00:48:20,449 --> 00:48:22,347
I think we missed her.

1215
00:48:22,382 --> 00:48:24,177
Maybe she's back
there someplace.

1216
00:48:24,211 --> 00:48:25,695
-Wait a minute.

1217
00:48:25,730 --> 00:48:28,629
♪♪

1218
00:48:28,664 --> 00:48:30,424
Tracks.

1219
00:48:30,459 --> 00:48:32,426
She's been right
around here someplace.

1220
00:48:32,461 --> 00:48:35,222
-Like right where
these tracks were made?

1221
00:48:35,257 --> 00:48:37,155
-D-Do you think she fell in?

1222
00:48:37,190 --> 00:48:38,191
I mean,

1223
00:48:38,225 --> 00:48:41,194
do the Centaurians
know how to swim?

1224
00:48:41,228 --> 00:48:42,885
♪♪

1225
00:48:42,920 --> 00:48:44,369
-I don't know.

1226
00:48:44,404 --> 00:48:49,374
♪♪

1227
00:48:49,409 --> 00:48:51,204
Her boot!

1228
00:48:51,238 --> 00:48:52,550
-Dibs, dibs.
-Das boot.

1229
00:48:52,584 --> 00:48:55,311
-Let me drink champagne
out of it, just to be sure.

1230
00:48:55,346 --> 00:48:58,314
♪♪

1231
00:48:58,349 --> 00:48:59,798
-Look at the size of that thing.

1232
00:48:59,833 --> 00:49:02,111
-Oh, I've seen trees
bigger than that.

1233
00:49:02,146 --> 00:49:03,699
That's nothing.
-Let's get back to the ship.

1234
00:49:03,733 --> 00:49:05,425
Let's go.
Come on, come on, come on.

1235
00:49:05,459 --> 00:49:08,738
♪♪

1236
00:49:08,773 --> 00:49:10,913
-We've been pacing
this studio for hours.

1237
00:49:10,948 --> 00:49:13,226
When do we get
some action happening?

1238
00:49:13,260 --> 00:49:20,233
♪♪

1239
00:49:20,267 --> 00:49:27,205
♪♪

1240
00:49:27,240 --> 00:49:30,070
-Hmm, coconut cream pie,

1241
00:49:30,105 --> 00:49:32,348
car made out of bamboo,

1242
00:49:32,383 --> 00:49:34,005
and a sailor's hat.

1243
00:49:34,040 --> 00:49:36,007
What's it all mean?

1244
00:49:36,042 --> 00:49:42,289
♪♪

1245
00:49:42,324 --> 00:49:43,911
-Alright.
Let's take five.

1246
00:49:43,946 --> 00:49:47,053
-1, 2, 3, 4, 5!
[Laughs] Psych!

1247
00:49:47,087 --> 00:49:52,403
♪♪

1248
00:49:52,437 --> 00:49:55,026
-What's a'matter, Bradley?
This hike too much for ya?

1249
00:49:55,061 --> 00:49:57,615
-Oh, no, Doc, I always
enjoy a 40-mile walk

1250
00:49:57,649 --> 00:50:00,031
on a lovely day like this.

1251
00:50:00,066 --> 00:50:02,551
-You ever see the movie
"Alive," doughnut boy?

1252
00:50:02,585 --> 00:50:04,932
-...you were 10 pounds
overweight.

1253
00:50:04,967 --> 00:50:06,003
-Are you kiddin', Doc?

1254
00:50:06,037 --> 00:50:07,659
I've been on a diet
for seven days,

1255
00:50:07,694 --> 00:50:09,489
and so far,
all I've lost is a week.

1256
00:50:09,523 --> 00:50:10,559
-Good one.

1257
00:50:10,593 --> 00:50:12,526
-And your self-respect
for that joke.

1258
00:50:12,561 --> 00:50:14,218
-Yeah.
That's the story of my life.

1259
00:50:14,252 --> 00:50:16,565
I'm always volunteering
for something.

1260
00:50:16,599 --> 00:50:18,153
I remember one day,
back in headquarters,

1261
00:50:18,187 --> 00:50:20,224
I volunteered
for a karate course.

1262
00:50:20,258 --> 00:50:22,088
They taught me all the chops
to the head, the neck,

1263
00:50:22,122 --> 00:50:24,021
the shoulders, the stomach --
the whole thing there.

1264
00:50:24,055 --> 00:50:25,401
Karate kicks -- hey!

1265
00:50:25,436 --> 00:50:27,024
-Now, who are you?
Donald O'Connor?

1266
00:50:27,058 --> 00:50:29,578
-Hi-keeba! Ha!

1267
00:50:29,612 --> 00:50:31,856
-You know,
I could watch that all day.

1268
00:50:31,890 --> 00:50:34,134
Do that again, will ya.

1269
00:50:34,169 --> 00:50:36,412
-And after
completing the course,

1270
00:50:36,447 --> 00:50:38,069
I became a karate expert.

1271
00:50:38,104 --> 00:50:39,829
-When did he become an idiot?

1272
00:50:39,864 --> 00:50:42,867
-I went around breaking boards
with my bare hand.

1273
00:50:42,901 --> 00:50:45,835
The first time
I saluted Admiral King,

1274
00:50:45,870 --> 00:50:47,285
I almost knocked my brains out.

1275
00:50:47,320 --> 00:50:48,838
-Sure you didn't succeed?
-I had to volunteer for this,

1276
00:50:48,873 --> 00:50:51,013
this mission to Centaurus,
and look where it got us.

1277
00:50:51,048 --> 00:50:52,635
-This guy's a hoot.
-Well, like you always told me,

1278
00:50:52,670 --> 00:50:54,913
Lieutenant, where's
your spirit of adventure?

1279
00:50:54,948 --> 00:50:56,881
-I left it in my other pants.

1280
00:50:56,915 --> 00:50:58,779
-That's what makes all
of this worthwhile --

1281
00:50:58,814 --> 00:51:00,057
the adventure...

1282
00:51:00,091 --> 00:51:01,851
day after day,
week after week,

1283
00:51:01,886 --> 00:51:03,819
month after month,
year after year.

1284
00:51:03,853 --> 00:51:05,407
All the sheer thrill
of it all --

1285
00:51:05,441 --> 00:51:06,925
perusing the planets,

1286
00:51:06,960 --> 00:51:08,755
unifying the universe,

1287
00:51:08,789 --> 00:51:10,619
galloping through the galactics.

1288
00:51:10,653 --> 00:51:12,379
-All: Shut up!

1289
00:51:12,414 --> 00:51:15,244
-...we had to the planet Gage
if I live to be normal.

1290
00:51:15,279 --> 00:51:16,349
We landed our ship in the middle

1291
00:51:16,383 --> 00:51:17,798
of the densest part
of the jungle,

1292
00:51:17,833 --> 00:51:19,628
and we were about 200 yards
from the ship when...

1293
00:51:19,662 --> 00:51:21,630
-Morty Gunty appearing this week

1294
00:51:21,664 --> 00:51:24,805
at the Blaine Community Theater
in Shakespeare's "King Lear."

1295
00:51:24,840 --> 00:51:26,152
-...from head to toe,

1296
00:51:26,186 --> 00:51:27,739
with fire and flame shooting

1297
00:51:27,774 --> 00:51:29,672
out of all three nostrils.

1298
00:51:29,707 --> 00:51:31,398
Well, I raised my laser gun...

1299
00:51:31,433 --> 00:51:33,676
-Shut him up.
-...activating mechanism.

1300
00:51:33,711 --> 00:51:34,919
The gun wouldn't work.

1301
00:51:34,953 --> 00:51:36,127
-I can't shut him up.

1302
00:51:36,162 --> 00:51:37,266
-It looked like certain
death for all of us.

1303
00:51:37,301 --> 00:51:39,130
-I can't reach his mouth.

1304
00:51:39,165 --> 00:51:41,546
-I reached back, and I grabbed
a handful of yours,

1305
00:51:41,581 --> 00:51:43,548
and I threw it right in
the beast's face and killed it.

1306
00:51:43,583 --> 00:51:45,516
-Now just a minute, Bradley.

1307
00:51:45,550 --> 00:51:48,001
You reached back
and grabbed a handful of what?

1308
00:51:48,035 --> 00:51:49,589
-Yours.
A handful of yours.

1309
00:51:49,623 --> 00:51:51,211
And I threw it right in --
-O-Okay. Okay, Bradley,

1310
00:51:51,246 --> 00:51:53,040
Tell me, what's yours?

1311
00:51:53,075 --> 00:51:54,697
-Uh, I'll have
a three-day pass, sir --

1312
00:51:54,732 --> 00:51:56,147
-Ba-boom!
-I'll kill him!

1313
00:51:56,182 --> 00:51:58,080
-Ba-ba-boom!
-I shouldn't have asked.

1314
00:51:58,115 --> 00:52:01,842
-[Laughs]
-Let's move out, shall we?

1315
00:52:01,877 --> 00:52:03,568
-Uh, he'll be
riding in the trunk

1316
00:52:03,603 --> 00:52:05,639
on the way back to Earth.

1317
00:52:05,674 --> 00:52:08,159
-He's a real child of hell.

1318
00:52:08,194 --> 00:52:13,544
♪♪

1319
00:52:13,578 --> 00:52:18,997
♪♪

1320
00:52:19,032 --> 00:52:21,483
-I gotta get a new log out here
or somethin'.

1321
00:52:21,517 --> 00:52:23,485
[Monkey chitters]

1322
00:52:23,519 --> 00:52:28,593
♪♪

1323
00:52:28,628 --> 00:52:33,736
♪♪

1324
00:52:33,771 --> 00:52:36,083
-Ah, Wilma, you're up.

1325
00:52:36,118 --> 00:52:44,678
♪♪

1326
00:52:44,713 --> 00:52:47,509
-Have you met Steve, my monkey?

1327
00:52:47,543 --> 00:52:49,856
♪♪

1328
00:52:49,890 --> 00:52:51,444
-Well, you got
some flaking there,

1329
00:52:51,478 --> 00:52:54,240
and you could use a henna.

1330
00:52:54,274 --> 00:52:56,932
-Pretty girl.

1331
00:52:56,966 --> 00:52:59,831
-Wh-Who are you?

1332
00:52:59,866 --> 00:53:01,143
-I am Tang.

1333
00:53:01,178 --> 00:53:02,731
-I'm not just
for breakfast anymore.

1334
00:53:02,765 --> 00:53:04,146
-That's my favorite drink!

1335
00:53:04,181 --> 00:53:06,666
-It's short for
Australopithecus africanus.

1336
00:53:06,700 --> 00:53:08,806
-I am Centaurian.

1337
00:53:08,840 --> 00:53:11,947
-Tang is my favorite drink.

1338
00:53:11,981 --> 00:53:14,018
-You are Centaurian.

1339
00:53:14,052 --> 00:53:16,020
We are same.

1340
00:53:16,054 --> 00:53:17,987
♪♪

1341
00:53:18,022 --> 00:53:20,404
-Well, how did you get here?

1342
00:53:20,438 --> 00:53:22,302
On this planet?

1343
00:53:22,337 --> 00:53:24,131
-It is my homeland.

1344
00:53:24,166 --> 00:53:26,789
♪♪

1345
00:53:26,824 --> 00:53:29,309
-You were born here?

1346
00:53:29,344 --> 00:53:31,656
Your mother was aboard
the Cosmos III.

1347
00:53:31,691 --> 00:53:35,591
-And your mother swimmed
after troop ships, lady.

1348
00:53:35,626 --> 00:53:37,248
-Survivors?

1349
00:53:37,283 --> 00:53:38,249
-Yes.

1350
00:53:38,284 --> 00:53:40,907
Your mother and father.

1351
00:53:40,941 --> 00:53:42,460
Where are they?

1352
00:53:44,359 --> 00:53:46,844
-Um, you're wearing them.

1353
00:53:46,878 --> 00:53:49,329
♪♪

1354
00:53:49,364 --> 00:53:51,331
-I will take you to them.

1355
00:53:51,366 --> 00:53:56,267
♪♪

1356
00:53:56,302 --> 00:53:58,511
-Uh, Tang...
-Uh, fresh out.

1357
00:53:58,545 --> 00:54:00,720
But I've got Catawba juice.

1358
00:54:00,754 --> 00:54:03,826
-Clothes wet.
You fall in water.

1359
00:54:03,861 --> 00:54:06,450
-Oh, yes, I remember.

1360
00:54:06,484 --> 00:54:08,383
There was a serpent.

1361
00:54:08,417 --> 00:54:11,903
-I kill serpent.
-Here, eat this hot stick.

1362
00:54:11,938 --> 00:54:13,905
You'll like it.

1363
00:54:13,940 --> 00:54:17,046
♪♪

1364
00:54:17,081 --> 00:54:19,532
-Leave the shower curtain here.

1365
00:54:19,566 --> 00:54:22,707
♪♪

1366
00:54:22,742 --> 00:54:24,571
-Oh, by the way,
I did that totem pole

1367
00:54:24,606 --> 00:54:26,642
just 'cause I thought
I was supposed to.

1368
00:54:26,677 --> 00:54:29,680
♪♪

1369
00:54:29,714 --> 00:54:31,406
-How much per month for this...

1370
00:54:31,440 --> 00:54:33,408
with the damage deposit?

1371
00:54:33,442 --> 00:54:40,587
♪♪

1372
00:54:40,622 --> 00:54:42,555
-[Speaking indistinctly]

1373
00:54:42,589 --> 00:54:52,150
♪♪

1374
00:54:52,185 --> 00:54:54,152
-Gong.

1375
00:54:54,187 --> 00:54:56,223
♪♪

1376
00:54:56,258 --> 00:54:59,537
-Now join me
in my dance of death.

1377
00:54:59,572 --> 00:55:07,994
♪♪

1378
00:55:08,028 --> 00:55:09,651
-That's cold.
-It is Zenda.

1379
00:55:09,685 --> 00:55:11,653
She's beautiful.

1380
00:55:11,687 --> 00:55:13,379
-Mother.
-She's not bad,

1381
00:55:13,413 --> 00:55:15,519
except for a little freezer burnaround the edges.

1382
00:55:15,553 --> 00:55:19,212
-It's Clarence Birdseye.
-That is Lieutenant Anderson!

1383
00:55:19,246 --> 00:55:21,041
He's not a Centaurian.

1384
00:55:21,076 --> 00:55:24,355
-My father.

1385
00:55:24,390 --> 00:55:26,909
-Well, they're now
a bowling trophy.

1386
00:55:26,944 --> 00:55:29,498
I must tell the admiral
about this.

1387
00:55:29,533 --> 00:55:32,639
I must go back to the ship
that brought me here.

1388
00:55:32,674 --> 00:55:35,366
Do you know where the ship is,
Tang?

1389
00:55:35,401 --> 00:55:37,644
-I know.
I take you there.

1390
00:55:37,679 --> 00:55:39,819
-And then I'll get
some pronouns.

1391
00:55:39,853 --> 00:55:41,579
♪♪

1392
00:55:57,768 --> 00:56:00,909
[Water bubbling]

1393
00:56:00,943 --> 00:56:03,739
-Meanwhile,
back at the dry ice pool...

1394
00:56:03,774 --> 00:56:13,266
♪♪

1395
00:56:13,300 --> 00:56:15,579
-[Sighs]
What a smell!

1396
00:56:15,613 --> 00:56:18,582
-Smell?
-Oh, that's just me.

1397
00:56:18,616 --> 00:56:20,963
-Bottle that muck,
you have a great secret weapon.

1398
00:56:20,998 --> 00:56:22,862
-Yeah, they could
use it for deodorant.

1399
00:56:22,896 --> 00:56:23,828
You rub it on,

1400
00:56:23,863 --> 00:56:25,623
the smell stays,
but you disappear.

1401
00:56:25,658 --> 00:56:26,762
-Good one.

1402
00:56:26,797 --> 00:56:28,074
-It's boiling crude oil,
Commander.

1403
00:56:28,108 --> 00:56:30,283
-Boy, what a spot
for a steam bath.

1404
00:56:30,317 --> 00:56:32,837
-Just cross the log,
and you're in the club.

1405
00:56:32,872 --> 00:56:34,183
-Hand me the rope.

1406
00:56:34,218 --> 00:56:36,669
♪♪

1407
00:56:36,703 --> 00:56:39,292
-You could walk around.

1408
00:56:39,326 --> 00:56:41,915
-Too easy.

1409
00:56:41,950 --> 00:56:45,574
Sure glad the prop boys laid
this log out here for us.

1410
00:56:45,609 --> 00:56:51,649
♪♪

1411
00:56:51,684 --> 00:56:53,996
-If the Wallendas can do it,
we can do it.

1412
00:56:54,031 --> 00:56:56,136
-...more nerve
than I thought he had.

1413
00:56:56,171 --> 00:56:57,621
-Scott's a good man, Bradley.

1414
00:56:57,655 --> 00:56:59,416
-He has wonderful
bone structure,

1415
00:56:59,450 --> 00:57:01,763
great teeth,
and a darling figure.

1416
00:57:01,797 --> 00:57:03,385
-Yeah, but what
a miserable place

1417
00:57:03,420 --> 00:57:06,181
to have to search in.
-Sir, he's right over there.

1418
00:57:06,215 --> 00:57:08,770
-A hazardous mission
is like the mirror of truth.

1419
00:57:08,804 --> 00:57:11,842
When you face it,
you see your true self.

1420
00:57:11,876 --> 00:57:12,912
-Oh, shut up.

1421
00:57:12,946 --> 00:57:14,016
What are you, Confucius?

1422
00:57:14,051 --> 00:57:17,503
Just cross the dry ice
and shut up!

1423
00:57:17,537 --> 00:57:19,366
-Why don't you build
a better bridge?

1424
00:57:19,401 --> 00:57:20,747
-Wrong kind of engineers.

1425
00:57:20,782 --> 00:57:24,268
-Uh, you see, uh...
we just play with toy trains.

1426
00:57:24,302 --> 00:57:26,857
-Frankly, yeah.

1427
00:57:26,891 --> 00:57:28,548
-Doc, I don't think
Allen better try it.

1428
00:57:28,583 --> 00:57:30,447
-Might want to take the slack
out of that rope.

1429
00:57:30,481 --> 00:57:32,069
-Allen, you stay behind.

1430
00:57:32,103 --> 00:57:33,484
-You little panty-waist boy.

1431
00:57:33,519 --> 00:57:37,005
If that was my kid,
I'd whack him!

1432
00:57:37,039 --> 00:57:38,316
-I'm okay, Doc. If, uh --

1433
00:57:38,351 --> 00:57:39,939
If you can make it, I can.

1434
00:57:39,973 --> 00:57:41,837
-Suit yourself.

1435
00:57:41,872 --> 00:57:44,012
-He's got it secure.
Let's go.

1436
00:57:44,046 --> 00:57:46,014
-He's got tequila?

1437
00:57:46,048 --> 00:57:50,190
♪♪

1438
00:57:50,225 --> 00:57:51,329
-Easy.

1439
00:57:51,364 --> 00:57:53,849
-My blood pressure
is just racing.

1440
00:57:53,884 --> 00:57:57,853
-Why don't they just
go around right there?

1441
00:57:57,888 --> 00:57:59,579
-I'm coming too, Chief!
-Wait, Allen!

1442
00:57:59,614 --> 00:58:02,306
Don't follow so close!
-Don't fall.

1443
00:58:02,340 --> 00:58:04,446
Don't fall.

1444
00:58:04,481 --> 00:58:06,690
Don't fall.
-Jinx! Jinx!

1445
00:58:06,724 --> 00:58:08,001
-[Indistinct shouting]
-[Indistinct shouting]

1446
00:58:08,036 --> 00:58:09,347
-Jinx.
-[Indistinct shouting]

1447
00:58:09,382 --> 00:58:10,210
-Jinx.
-Don't fall.

1448
00:58:10,245 --> 00:58:11,695
Don't fall.
-[Trills tongue]

1449
00:58:11,729 --> 00:58:12,799
-Miss.
-Chicken chicken.

1450
00:58:12,834 --> 00:58:13,731
-[Screams]
-Ooh, shame.

1451
00:58:13,766 --> 00:58:16,562
What a pity.
-Help!

1452
00:58:16,596 --> 00:58:18,253
-Hot foot.
-It's gainer.

1453
00:58:18,287 --> 00:58:19,634
-Yes, it is.

1454
00:58:19,668 --> 00:58:22,809
-Well, I guess
he's not in the club.

1455
00:58:22,844 --> 00:58:24,811
-Never did like him anyway.

1456
00:58:24,846 --> 00:58:26,364
-That's right.

1457
00:58:26,399 --> 00:58:27,400
Lousy.

1458
00:58:27,434 --> 00:58:28,988
That's what
this whole mission is.

1459
00:58:29,022 --> 00:58:31,542
-Can I have his locker?
-Lousy.

1460
00:58:31,577 --> 00:58:33,751
What the hell are
we trying to prove, anyway?

1461
00:58:33,786 --> 00:58:35,408
Searching a stinkin'
nowhere planet

1462
00:58:35,442 --> 00:58:37,444
for a bunch of survivors
that may not exist?

1463
00:58:37,479 --> 00:58:39,654
-Hey, I got Angelo to think of.

1464
00:58:39,688 --> 00:58:41,966
-Take it easy, Chief.

1465
00:58:42,001 --> 00:58:43,554
-Take it easy?

1466
00:58:43,589 --> 00:58:46,384
You watch a man die like that,
and you say, "Take it easy?"

1467
00:58:46,419 --> 00:58:47,903
-Hey, I thought it was funny.

1468
00:58:47,938 --> 00:58:50,423
Why?
-[Gasps]

1469
00:58:50,457 --> 00:58:53,460
-You alright now, Chief?

1470
00:58:53,495 --> 00:58:55,497
-Yeah.
Thanks for not hitting me.

1471
00:58:55,532 --> 00:58:57,257
-Sorry, sir.

1472
00:58:57,292 --> 00:58:59,881
Something just happened to me.

1473
00:58:59,915 --> 00:59:02,608
Got a little shook, I guess.

1474
00:59:02,642 --> 00:59:04,092
-Forget it, Chief.

1475
00:59:04,126 --> 00:59:05,196
We're all a little shook.

1476
00:59:05,231 --> 00:59:06,543
-Dickweed.

1477
00:59:06,577 --> 00:59:08,199
-Now, we're almost there.

1478
00:59:08,234 --> 00:59:10,408
Let's move on.

1479
00:59:10,443 --> 00:59:11,582
-Dork.

1480
00:59:11,617 --> 00:59:15,586
♪♪

1481
00:59:15,621 --> 00:59:17,554
-Come on, son.
-I know how you're feeling.

1482
00:59:17,588 --> 00:59:18,693
You see this shoulder?

1483
00:59:18,727 --> 00:59:21,074
It's here if you need it.

1484
00:59:21,109 --> 00:59:23,456
Idiot.

1485
00:59:23,490 --> 00:59:26,114
-Now, would you go back across
the log and unhook the rope?

1486
00:59:26,148 --> 00:59:28,565
-That makes me hungry.

1487
00:59:28,599 --> 00:59:30,946
-Is he primordial soup yet?

1488
00:59:30,981 --> 00:59:35,606
♪♪

1489
00:59:35,641 --> 00:59:37,781
-Now, where did we park?

1490
00:59:37,815 --> 00:59:42,026
♪♪

1491
00:59:42,061 --> 00:59:43,441
-More of the same.

1492
00:59:43,476 --> 00:59:44,753
Oh, brother.

1493
00:59:44,788 --> 00:59:50,932
♪♪

1494
00:59:50,966 --> 00:59:53,072
-Who's line is it?

1495
00:59:53,106 --> 00:59:54,591
-Somebody.

1496
00:59:54,625 --> 01:00:02,184
♪♪

1497
01:00:02,219 --> 01:00:04,842
-Someone's gotta have a line.
There we are.

1498
01:00:04,877 --> 01:00:06,361
-Kinda sickening, isn't it?

1499
01:00:06,395 --> 01:00:07,949
Nobody could've survived
this crash.

1500
01:00:07,983 --> 01:00:10,020
-Bradley,
you and the chief wait here.

1501
01:00:10,054 --> 01:00:12,988
We can't be sure
what we might find inside.

1502
01:00:13,023 --> 01:00:15,266
With me, Doc?

1503
01:00:15,301 --> 01:00:18,166
-Yeah, you two disposable
characters wait here.

1504
01:00:18,200 --> 01:00:26,174
♪♪

1505
01:00:26,208 --> 01:00:28,348
-Wow.

1506
01:00:28,383 --> 01:00:31,766
Looks like they turned
that ship into a restaurant.

1507
01:00:31,800 --> 01:00:33,837
I love these fern bars.

1508
01:00:33,871 --> 01:00:35,977
Looks like
an interstellar Denny's.

1509
01:00:36,011 --> 01:00:38,669
It's great.

1510
01:00:38,704 --> 01:00:41,361
-Wow. They had
more switches than we do.

1511
01:00:41,396 --> 01:00:46,815
♪♪

1512
01:00:46,850 --> 01:00:49,542
-Did you float an air biscuit
or is that me?

1513
01:00:49,576 --> 01:00:53,201
♪♪

1514
01:00:53,235 --> 01:00:54,581
-Captain Ross.

1515
01:00:54,616 --> 01:00:56,583
-Hey, their doggy left
all their bones here.

1516
01:00:56,618 --> 01:00:58,240
-That's the people, you dolt.

1517
01:00:58,275 --> 01:00:59,586
-Oh.

1518
01:00:59,621 --> 01:01:02,969
♪♪

1519
01:01:03,004 --> 01:01:04,108
-Nice wallet.

1520
01:01:04,143 --> 01:01:07,767
-That's his sternum.
Don't touch it.

1521
01:01:07,802 --> 01:01:09,769
-That we know of.

1522
01:01:09,804 --> 01:01:11,012
-[Barks]

1523
01:01:11,046 --> 01:01:14,222
-I want to check every
compartment in this ship.

1524
01:01:14,256 --> 01:01:15,879
Ask Bradley and the chief
to do it.

1525
01:01:15,913 --> 01:01:17,708
Get 'em back here.

1526
01:01:17,743 --> 01:01:25,647
♪♪

1527
01:01:25,682 --> 01:01:26,855
-Huh.

1528
01:01:26,890 --> 01:01:28,926
-It says, "Thou shalt not k--"

1529
01:01:28,961 --> 01:01:30,721
-That was in the other show.

1530
01:01:30,756 --> 01:01:31,584
-Oh, yeah.

1531
01:01:31,618 --> 01:01:33,413
-It's a closin' play.

1532
01:01:33,448 --> 01:01:37,797
♪♪

1533
01:01:37,832 --> 01:01:40,317
-And says, "Ahab was obsessed

1534
01:01:40,351 --> 01:01:42,422
with the great white one."

1535
01:01:42,457 --> 01:01:44,770
♪♪

1536
01:01:44,804 --> 01:01:46,841
It's a well! There's
a little girl down there!

1537
01:01:46,875 --> 01:01:49,360
-Well, it's the same situation
down in the maneuvering room.

1538
01:01:49,395 --> 01:01:51,017
The entire engineering crew.

1539
01:01:51,052 --> 01:01:51,984
-The maneuvering room?

1540
01:01:52,018 --> 01:01:54,987
Don't you mean the bathroom?

1541
01:01:55,021 --> 01:01:57,023
-It's like a tomb.

1542
01:01:57,058 --> 01:01:58,715
-You know, there's one thing
that puzzles me, Doc.

1543
01:01:58,749 --> 01:02:01,752
-Fractions.-...hatch is left standing open.

1544
01:02:01,787 --> 01:02:03,029
-Huh?
-Huh?

1545
01:02:03,064 --> 01:02:04,341
-And, uh, looks likesome of them have been forced...

1546
01:02:04,375 --> 01:02:06,136
-Kinda like
your characterization.

1547
01:02:06,170 --> 01:02:08,655
-So someone else has
searched through the ship?

1548
01:02:08,690 --> 01:02:10,727
-Yeah, something like that.

1549
01:02:17,492 --> 01:02:19,701
-We've accounted for all
but five.

1550
01:02:22,083 --> 01:02:24,119
-There were five survivors.

1551
01:02:24,154 --> 01:02:26,328
-You mean that recorder
still works after 18 years?

1552
01:02:26,363 --> 01:02:27,709
-Yeah, but it's on an 8-track.

1553
01:02:27,744 --> 01:02:30,608
-Emergency power pack is as goodas the day it was installed.

1554
01:02:30,643 --> 01:02:32,162
Listen to this.

1555
01:02:34,474 --> 01:02:36,200
-And now there are
only three of us,

1556
01:02:36,235 --> 01:02:37,892
and I don't expect
Sergeant Long to last

1557
01:02:37,926 --> 01:02:39,617
more than a few days now.

1558
01:02:39,652 --> 01:02:40,964
-Long won't last long.

1559
01:02:40,998 --> 01:02:43,656
-...caused a loss of sight
in both eyes,

1560
01:02:43,690 --> 01:02:46,970
and his brain seems
to have been damaged somehow.

1561
01:02:47,004 --> 01:02:50,318
I'm also certain that my fever
will soon overcome me.

1562
01:02:50,352 --> 01:02:52,044
There are no more drugs
aboard the ship.

1563
01:02:52,078 --> 01:02:54,563
-This planet's totally dry,
dudes.

1564
01:02:54,598 --> 01:02:56,738
Don't look to be
scorin' nothin' here.

1565
01:02:56,773 --> 01:02:59,534
-Year 1, 74th day.

1566
01:02:59,568 --> 01:03:00,500
[Recorder clicks]

1567
01:03:00,535 --> 01:03:02,054
-That's the last report?

1568
01:03:02,088 --> 01:03:05,264
-74 long days...

1569
01:03:05,298 --> 01:03:07,404
watching the sky...

1570
01:03:07,438 --> 01:03:09,958
waiting for us.

1571
01:03:09,993 --> 01:03:12,754
Waiting for death.

1572
01:03:12,789 --> 01:03:14,100
-Nice speech, Jim.

1573
01:03:14,135 --> 01:03:17,276
-Anderson knew it would
take us 18 years to get back.

1574
01:03:17,310 --> 01:03:18,829
The important thing is,

1575
01:03:18,864 --> 01:03:20,451
he knew we would.

1576
01:03:20,486 --> 01:03:22,764
-Thank God we did.

1577
01:03:22,799 --> 01:03:25,353
-You think they might be alive
up in the mountains?

1578
01:03:25,387 --> 01:03:26,354
-It's possible.

1579
01:03:26,388 --> 01:03:28,114
-[Screaming]

1580
01:03:28,149 --> 01:03:30,358
-Jim's constipated.

1581
01:03:30,392 --> 01:03:31,600
-[Screaming continues]

1582
01:03:31,635 --> 01:03:33,464
-Hey, Chief, what's wrong?

1583
01:03:33,499 --> 01:03:34,500
-[Screams]

1584
01:03:34,534 --> 01:03:36,191
-What's wrong with him, Doc?

1585
01:03:36,226 --> 01:03:38,159
-I don't know.

1586
01:03:38,193 --> 01:03:39,850
-Why don't you ask him?

1587
01:03:39,885 --> 01:03:42,680
♪♪

1588
01:03:42,715 --> 01:03:44,337
-Quick, get back!

1589
01:03:44,372 --> 01:03:46,961
♪♪

1590
01:03:46,995 --> 01:03:51,655
-Oh, my God, how horrible,
to be killed by a plush toy!

1591
01:03:51,689 --> 01:03:52,829
-Tarantula.

1592
01:03:52,863 --> 01:03:54,451
Don't make any quick moves.

1593
01:03:54,485 --> 01:03:56,798
Doc, keep perfectly still.

1594
01:03:56,833 --> 01:03:58,558
Can I have your laser gun.

1595
01:03:58,593 --> 01:03:59,525
-Yes, sir.

1596
01:03:59,559 --> 01:04:01,423
-Kinda late now, isn't it?

1597
01:04:01,458 --> 01:04:06,635
♪♪

1598
01:04:06,670 --> 01:04:09,397
-Careful, Scott, he sees you.
He can spring that far.

1599
01:04:09,431 --> 01:04:10,536
-We rigged him that way.

1600
01:04:10,570 --> 01:04:11,882
-Look out!
He's gonna jump.

1601
01:04:11,917 --> 01:04:13,746
[High-pitched whining]

1602
01:04:13,780 --> 01:04:16,645
-Nice ricochet sound.

1603
01:04:16,680 --> 01:04:20,132
-The hopping spider of Rylos 4.

1604
01:04:20,166 --> 01:04:22,651
-Well, I guess
he's really an angel now.

1605
01:04:22,686 --> 01:04:23,652
-Yep.

1606
01:04:23,687 --> 01:04:26,655
-He died more from shock
than the poison.

1607
01:04:26,690 --> 01:04:28,140
-Little leech, I guess.

1608
01:04:28,174 --> 01:04:30,142
-Quick diagnosis, though.

1609
01:04:30,176 --> 01:04:32,938
-Let's go.

1610
01:04:32,972 --> 01:04:35,630
-Well, don't
everybody be so sad.

1611
01:04:35,664 --> 01:04:40,773
♪♪

1612
01:04:40,807 --> 01:04:45,916
♪♪

1613
01:04:45,951 --> 01:04:47,849
-What else is on the tape?

1614
01:04:47,884 --> 01:04:49,851
-Uh, Eddie Money,
"Two Tickets to Paradise."

1615
01:04:49,886 --> 01:04:51,991
That's about it.
And the DeFranco Family.

1616
01:04:52,026 --> 01:04:53,441
I don't think you'd like it.

1617
01:04:53,475 --> 01:04:55,684
-Two of them did.

1618
01:04:55,719 --> 01:04:58,377
They murdered Ross.

1619
01:04:58,411 --> 01:05:00,724
-This'll hit the admiral
pretty hard.

1620
01:05:00,758 --> 01:05:01,967
-Yeah.

1621
01:05:02,001 --> 01:05:03,416
-Hit them pretty hard, too, Doc.

1622
01:05:03,451 --> 01:05:05,936
-Wrecked 'em?
Damn near killed 'em.

1623
01:05:05,971 --> 01:05:07,834
-And so, Johnny Longtorso

1624
01:05:07,869 --> 01:05:10,734
and his industrial band
of bad-boy spacefaring pals

1625
01:05:10,768 --> 01:05:14,807
step out into the cold air
of Central Park.

1626
01:05:14,841 --> 01:05:19,225
♪♪

1627
01:05:46,459 --> 01:05:50,981
♪♪

1628
01:05:51,016 --> 01:05:52,465
-Oh...

1629
01:05:52,500 --> 01:05:56,055
♪♪

1630
01:05:56,090 --> 01:05:58,057
-Ahhhhh!
-They were your mothers'.

1631
01:05:58,092 --> 01:06:01,509
-Well, they were
my mother's shower curtains.

1632
01:06:01,543 --> 01:06:04,961
-Oh, yes, Tang.
-Crazy woman.

1633
01:06:04,995 --> 01:06:06,514
I offer her something
to drink --

1634
01:06:06,548 --> 01:06:09,517
She thinks it's my name
all of the sudden.

1635
01:06:09,551 --> 01:06:11,174
-What the...?

1636
01:06:11,208 --> 01:06:18,836
♪♪

1637
01:06:18,871 --> 01:06:21,667
-How long have your mother
and father been gone, Tang?

1638
01:06:21,701 --> 01:06:23,496
-You mean...frozen?

1639
01:06:23,531 --> 01:06:25,498
-I was child.

1640
01:06:25,533 --> 01:06:27,880
-Then who taught you to speak?

1641
01:06:27,914 --> 01:06:29,261
How'd you live?

1642
01:06:29,295 --> 01:06:30,917
-Mother teach.

1643
01:06:30,952 --> 01:06:32,057
She live until...

1644
01:06:32,091 --> 01:06:34,921
-She held me back
in the third grade.

1645
01:06:34,956 --> 01:06:36,647
♪♪

1646
01:06:36,682 --> 01:06:38,891
-Do you live here all alone?

1647
01:06:38,925 --> 01:06:40,065
-Yes.

1648
01:06:40,099 --> 01:06:41,204
Alone.

1649
01:06:41,238 --> 01:06:43,206
-If you don't count
my 27 monkeys.

1650
01:06:43,240 --> 01:06:45,104
-...people on this planet?

1651
01:06:45,139 --> 01:06:48,314
-Yes, there are others.

1652
01:06:48,349 --> 01:06:52,111
Savages who hate and kill.

1653
01:06:52,146 --> 01:06:54,148
They do not want Tang
to live in their country.

1654
01:06:54,182 --> 01:06:56,943
-Oh, you mean Crash Orange,
Goofy Grape, Lefty Lemon?

1655
01:06:56,978 --> 01:06:58,048
That whole gang?

1656
01:06:58,083 --> 01:07:00,257
-Only enemies.

1657
01:07:00,292 --> 01:07:03,674
-But there is room
for many people to live here.

1658
01:07:03,709 --> 01:07:05,159
Why should they object?

1659
01:07:05,193 --> 01:07:06,988
-Well, this is
the high-rent district,

1660
01:07:07,023 --> 01:07:09,059
and you know how people get.

1661
01:07:09,094 --> 01:07:12,062
They are primitive men.

1662
01:07:12,097 --> 01:07:13,132
-The producers?

1663
01:07:13,167 --> 01:07:15,203
-No, these men are different.

1664
01:07:15,238 --> 01:07:16,653
-Oh, the executive producers.

1665
01:07:16,687 --> 01:07:19,483
-Natch.
-...fear men who are different.

1666
01:07:19,518 --> 01:07:23,694
♪♪

1667
01:07:23,729 --> 01:07:24,868
-Oh, wow!

1668
01:07:24,902 --> 01:07:27,629
Mom never looked thatgood.

1669
01:07:27,664 --> 01:07:29,114
-Beautiful...

1670
01:07:29,148 --> 01:07:30,460
girl.

1671
01:07:30,494 --> 01:07:33,290
♪♪

1672
01:07:33,325 --> 01:07:35,120
-My name is Linda.

1673
01:07:35,154 --> 01:07:36,466
-My name is Lucky.

1674
01:07:36,500 --> 01:07:38,985
-Linda.

1675
01:07:39,020 --> 01:07:40,366
Tang.

1676
01:07:40,401 --> 01:07:44,198
-I'm not just for breakfast
anymore.

1677
01:07:44,232 --> 01:07:45,716
-Can I call you...

1678
01:07:45,751 --> 01:07:48,133
Ovaltine?

1679
01:07:48,167 --> 01:07:50,066
-I'll drink to that.

1680
01:07:52,344 --> 01:07:54,000
-Who undressed me?

1681
01:07:54,035 --> 01:07:55,140
-[Grunts]

1682
01:07:55,174 --> 01:07:56,141
-Linda's clothes wet.

1683
01:07:56,175 --> 01:07:57,659
I undress.

1684
01:07:57,694 --> 01:07:59,489
♪♪

1685
01:07:59,523 --> 01:08:02,112
-Nice tag, Tang.

1686
01:08:03,493 --> 01:08:06,289
-I did not mean to hurt you.

1687
01:08:06,323 --> 01:08:09,015
♪♪

1688
01:08:09,050 --> 01:08:11,846
-I can love only through pain.

1689
01:08:11,880 --> 01:08:14,159
♪♪

1690
01:08:14,193 --> 01:08:16,333
-Do not be afraid of me.

1691
01:08:16,368 --> 01:08:18,439
♪♪

1692
01:08:18,473 --> 01:08:21,269
-I am not afraid of you, Tang.

1693
01:08:21,304 --> 01:08:24,445
♪♪

1694
01:08:24,479 --> 01:08:27,275
-Bang-shangalang, Tang.

1695
01:08:27,310 --> 01:08:30,451
-Wang, bang, thank you, Tang.

1696
01:08:30,485 --> 01:08:32,625
♪♪

1697
01:08:32,660 --> 01:08:34,938
[Thunder crashes]

1698
01:08:34,972 --> 01:08:37,630
-♪ The weather started
getting rough ♪

1699
01:08:37,665 --> 01:08:40,875
♪ And Tang and she were tossed

1700
01:08:48,365 --> 01:08:51,541
-Zzz-z-z-z!
[Thunder crashes]

1701
01:08:51,575 --> 01:08:54,302
-Dean will get, uh,
lady and the plumbers

1702
01:08:54,337 --> 01:08:55,821
to do all the dirty work.

1703
01:08:55,855 --> 01:08:58,617
-Lieutenant Anderson,
navigation officer, Cosmos III.

1704
01:08:58,651 --> 01:09:01,137
-Hey, Crow, sounds like one
of the more boring episodes

1705
01:09:01,171 --> 01:09:02,586
of "Prairie Home Companion,"
huh?

1706
01:09:02,621 --> 01:09:04,588
-Yeah, let's get it on tape.

1707
01:09:04,623 --> 01:09:07,004
[Thunder crashes]

1708
01:09:07,039 --> 01:09:09,938
-Canyon's to the west.

1709
01:09:09,973 --> 01:09:13,735
That would place himin the upper end of this canyon.

1710
01:09:13,770 --> 01:09:16,945
Probably a cave
high in the cliffs.

1711
01:09:16,980 --> 01:09:18,809
Well, they'd be fairly safe
from the --

1712
01:09:18,844 --> 01:09:21,053
-I know what you mean!

1713
01:09:21,087 --> 01:09:22,296
-Admiral.

1714
01:09:22,330 --> 01:09:25,368
Do you seriously believe
any of them are still alive?

1715
01:09:25,402 --> 01:09:27,646
-Maybe I do. I don't know
what I think of that.

1716
01:09:27,680 --> 01:09:29,199
-Well, then, why haven't
they used the radio?

1717
01:09:29,234 --> 01:09:33,030
Or continue the tape report
beyond 74 days?

1718
01:09:33,065 --> 01:09:34,480
-He has a point, Admiral.

1719
01:09:34,515 --> 01:09:36,172
-Granted, but...

1720
01:09:36,206 --> 01:09:37,656
even if we find nothing --

1721
01:09:37,690 --> 01:09:39,830
-[Shouts in gibberish]
Talk like this, mm.

1722
01:09:39,865 --> 01:09:41,729
-...at least we'd be sure.

1723
01:09:43,731 --> 01:09:46,216
-Admiral King.

1724
01:09:46,251 --> 01:09:48,701
Are the dead worth more lives
than the living?

1725
01:09:48,736 --> 01:09:49,909
-What's a dead man

1726
01:09:49,944 --> 01:09:51,877
other than a live man
without any life?

1727
01:09:51,911 --> 01:09:54,051
-Sergeant Allen, the Chief...

1728
01:09:54,086 --> 01:09:55,260
Linda...

1729
01:09:55,294 --> 01:09:58,124
-I'm not convinced
that Linda is dead, either.

1730
01:09:58,159 --> 01:09:59,367
-[Grunting, mumbling]

1731
01:09:59,402 --> 01:10:01,749
-Might possibly be a captive.

1732
01:10:01,783 --> 01:10:03,268
-A captive?

1733
01:10:03,302 --> 01:10:04,752
-Harris and Wilson
said the serpent

1734
01:10:04,786 --> 01:10:08,618
was killed by a metal shaft...

1735
01:10:08,652 --> 01:10:10,136
possibly an arrow.

1736
01:10:10,171 --> 01:10:12,069
[Thunder crashes]

1737
01:10:12,104 --> 01:10:14,900
There might be primitive men
on this planet.

1738
01:10:14,934 --> 01:10:16,936
-"Men without pocket squares."

1739
01:10:16,971 --> 01:10:20,008
-Hey, did you just get back
from the dentist or something?

1740
01:10:20,043 --> 01:10:22,218
-Forewarning -- I'm going
to go out and find out.

1741
01:10:22,252 --> 01:10:23,529
-Uh...

1742
01:10:23,564 --> 01:10:26,498
-Did he just get
a shot of Novocain?

1743
01:10:26,532 --> 01:10:29,328
-JD.
-Goodnight, gentlemen.

1744
01:10:29,363 --> 01:10:31,399
[Thunder crashes]

1745
01:10:34,575 --> 01:10:37,267
-[Exhales sharply]
Is it my breath?

1746
01:10:45,655 --> 01:10:47,760
[Thunder crashes]

1747
01:10:47,795 --> 01:10:50,798
-Oh, Cap'n Crunch comfort ring,

1748
01:10:50,832 --> 01:10:53,456
help me out
of this awful jam now.

1749
01:10:53,490 --> 01:10:55,147
-Shazam!

1750
01:10:55,181 --> 01:10:58,978
♪♪

1751
01:10:59,013 --> 01:11:00,532
[Upbeat music plays]

1752
01:11:00,566 --> 01:11:02,706
-Yeah.

1753
01:11:02,741 --> 01:11:05,744
♪♪

1754
01:11:05,778 --> 01:11:07,366
-Go, Annette, go.

1755
01:11:07,401 --> 01:11:09,023
-How'd she get
that X-ray machine

1756
01:11:09,057 --> 01:11:11,612
to sound that good?

1757
01:11:11,646 --> 01:11:13,303
-The space samba.

1758
01:11:13,338 --> 01:11:14,960
♪♪

1759
01:11:14,994 --> 01:11:18,791
-Wow. She'd be perfect
for your experiments.

1760
01:11:18,826 --> 01:11:24,418
♪♪

1761
01:11:24,452 --> 01:11:26,247
-A little girl
is trapped down there,

1762
01:11:26,282 --> 01:11:28,974
and all you can do is dance.

1763
01:11:29,008 --> 01:11:30,493
It was murder.

1764
01:11:30,527 --> 01:11:32,357
-Ah, the sexual harassment laws

1765
01:11:32,391 --> 01:11:34,566
are a lot looser in the future.

1766
01:11:34,600 --> 01:11:37,914
-You know you love it.
-Yeah, but, uh, not when

1767
01:11:37,948 --> 01:11:40,157
we can't do anything about it.
-Six inches. Come on.

1768
01:11:40,192 --> 01:11:42,746
-Alright, break it up.

1769
01:11:42,781 --> 01:11:44,783
Mr. Bradley,
didn't you ever read

1770
01:11:44,817 --> 01:11:47,579
the regulations covering
shipboard romances?

1771
01:11:47,613 --> 01:11:49,305
-'Course I did,
but [laughs]

1772
01:11:49,339 --> 01:11:51,652
this started a long time
before we came aboard.

1773
01:11:51,686 --> 01:11:54,344
-I can only read braille.
-...get back home,

1774
01:11:54,379 --> 01:11:56,104
or you'll find yourself
with a busted ensign.

1775
01:11:56,139 --> 01:11:57,174
-A busted ensign.

1776
01:11:57,209 --> 01:11:58,728
We all know how painful
that can be.

1777
01:11:58,762 --> 01:12:00,523
-...on a sweet little
junior officer like this

1778
01:12:00,557 --> 01:12:02,663
just 'cause you're jealous?

1779
01:12:02,697 --> 01:12:07,150
-Bradley, there are some men whocan send me right into orbit.

1780
01:12:07,184 --> 01:12:09,704
You couldn't even get me
onto the launching pad.

1781
01:12:09,739 --> 01:12:11,465
-Yikes.
Nice tag.

1782
01:12:11,499 --> 01:12:13,363
-Mr. Scott!

1783
01:12:13,398 --> 01:12:15,158
-Oh, you're over there.
I didn't look.

1784
01:12:15,192 --> 01:12:17,678
-Why isn't my field gear ready?

1785
01:12:18,920 --> 01:12:20,301
Bradley!
-Yes, sir?

1786
01:12:20,336 --> 01:12:23,131
-Where's Mr. Scott?

1787
01:12:23,166 --> 01:12:25,168
Why isn't the search party
ready to leave?

1788
01:12:25,202 --> 01:12:26,134
-Mr. Scott...

1789
01:12:26,169 --> 01:12:27,377
-[Speaks gibberish]

1790
01:12:27,412 --> 01:12:28,758
-...Doc and Harris with him.

1791
01:12:28,792 --> 01:12:31,485
He -- He said you wouldn't
let anybody else go.

1792
01:12:31,519 --> 01:12:32,865
-Hmm.

1793
01:12:32,900 --> 01:12:35,040
He -- He said that, did he?
-I knew that.

1794
01:12:35,074 --> 01:12:38,250
-But those were your orders,
weren't they, Admiral?

1795
01:12:38,284 --> 01:12:39,216
-Yes.
-Hmm.

1796
01:12:39,251 --> 01:12:40,528
-Yes, of course.
-Yes, of course.

1797
01:12:40,563 --> 01:12:41,667
Hmm.

1798
